.SH DESCRIPTION
.B socketley
is a high-performance Linux daemon and CLI that manages network runtimes
(servers, clients, proxies, Redis-compatible caches) in a Docker-like style \(em
with clustering, WebSocket, Lua scripting, and io_uring async I/O.
.PP
.B "TLS terminates on a server listener, on both of a proxy's legs, and on a client's dial."
A
.B server
runtime encrypts its own listening port with
.BR \-\-tls " " \-\-cert " " \-\-key ,
and verifies client certificates with
.BR \-\-ca .
A
.B proxy
does the same on its own port, verifies client certificates with
.BR \-\-client\-ca ,
and reaches an
.B https://
backend over TLS \(em verifying the chain and the endpoint's name, and
presenting a client certificate with
.BR \-\-client\-cert " " \-\-client\-key .
A
.B client
runtime dials its target over TLS with
.BR \-\-tls ,
verifying the chain against
.B \-\-ca
or the system trust store and matching the host from
.B \-t
against the certificate; it presents its own certificate with
.BR \-\-cert " " \-\-key .
There is no way to switch either check off \(em an unverified TLS client buys
encryption against somebody recording the wire and nothing at all against
somebody sitting on it.
A
.B cache
terminates TLS on its own listener with the same three flags a server uses, and
RESP runs on the decrypted stream, so a Redis client needs no change. The one
thing it refuses is
.B \-\-tls
together with
.BR \-\-replicate :
replication is not encrypted, so the pair would put the whole dataset on the
wire in the clear. A runtime carrying a configuration that cannot be honoured
(for example one restored from a state file written by an older release)
refuses to start rather than running in cleartext. See
.B TLS
under
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.PP
The io_uring event loop automatically takes advantage of advanced kernel
features when available: multishot receive (one SQE services many reads),
zero-copy send (SEND_ZC \(em data goes straight from userspace to the NIC
without a kernel copy), and registered file descriptors (skip per-op
.BR fget / fput
overhead). Each feature is probed at startup and falls back transparently
to standard ops on older kernels, so no configuration is needed.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B daemon
Start the socketley daemon.
When installed system-wide (via .deb package or install.sh), the daemon is
managed by systemd and starts automatically \(em you do not need to run this
command manually. Use
.B systemctl start socketley
or
.B systemctl enable socketley
to control the service.
.PP
When running from the build directory (development mode), start the daemon
manually:
.B ./bin/Release/socketley daemon &
.PP
Creates IPC socket at /run/socketley/socketley.sock (system install) or
/tmp/socketley.sock (development mode).
On startup, restores persisted runtime configurations. Runtimes that were
running when the daemon last stopped are auto-started.
.PP
Takes
.BR \-\-sqpoll ,
.B \-\-no\-sqpoll
and
.BI \-\-cpus " set"
\(em the ring settings described under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" ,
here shaping the ring this daemon is about to create rather than stating what a
runtime requires of one. They are the command-line spelling of
.B SOCKETLEY_SQPOLL
and
.BR SOCKETLEY_CPUS ,
and a flag wins when both are given. Any other argument is refused: this command
used to ignore its command line entirely, so
.B socketley daemon \-\-sqpoll
started a daemon with no poller and exited 0.
.IP
They cannot be combined with
.B \-\-name
or
.BR \-\-cluster ,
which address a daemon that is already running \(em a ring's flags are fixed when
the ring is created, so there would be nothing left for them to change.
.TP
.BI "daemon \-\-name " "name " "[\-\-cluster " "dir" "]"
Configure the running daemon for cluster mode. These flags are sent as IPC
commands to the already-running daemon (auto-started if not running).
.B \-\-name
sets the daemon's identity in the cluster.
.B \-\-cluster
enables cluster discovery on the given shared directory (see
.BR "CLUSTER MODE" ).
The name can be changed at any time; if cluster mode is active, the cluster
file is republished under the new name.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley daemon --name node1 --cluster /shared/cluster
socketley daemon --name new-name   # rename while cluster is active
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "create " "type name " "[flags]"
Create a new runtime instance.
.I type
can be: server, client, proxy, cache.
.I name
is a unique identifier for the runtime.
.TP
.BI "attach " "type name port " "[\-\-owner " "name" "]"
Register an external process as a runtime visible in
.BR ls / ps .
The daemon does NOT bind the port or forward any I/O — the external process
manages its own network connections. The runtime appears as running and can
be inspected or removed like any other runtime.
.RS
.TP
.I type
Runtime type: server, client, proxy, or cache.
.TP
.I name
Unique name for this external runtime.
.TP
.I port
Port number the external process is listening on (informational only).
.TP
.B \-\-owner \fIname\fR
Optional owner runtime name.
.RE
.TP
.BI "add " "path " "[\-\-name " "name" "] [\-s]"
Register and manage an external binary (built with the C++ SDK).
The daemon will fork+exec the binary and manage its full lifecycle:
auto-restart on crash, re-launch on daemon boot, and normal
start/stop/remove commands.
.RS
.TP
.I path
Path to the executable (must exist and be executable).
.TP
.B \-\-name \fIname\fR, \fB\-n\fR \fIname\fR
Name for the runtime. If omitted, derived from the binary's filename
(basename without extension).
.TP
.B \-s
Start immediately after adding.
.PP
The binary receives
.B SOCKETLEY_MANAGED=1
and
.B SOCKETLEY_NAME=\fIname\fR
environment variables. When using
.BR daemon_attach() ,
these cause the binary to re-attach to the existing runtime entry
and skip the atexit self-removal.
.PP
.nf
socketley add /usr/local/bin/myapp -s
socketley add /usr/local/bin/myapp --name chat
socketley stop chat
socketley start chat
socketley remove chat
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "start " "name|pattern" "... [\-i]"
Start one or more runtimes. Accepts multiple names, glob patterns
.RB ( * ", " ? ", " [] ),
or a mix. With
.BR * ,
starts all non-running runtimes.
.RS
.TP
.B \-i
Interactive mode. Starts the runtime and opens an interactive session.
For server: stdin is broadcast to clients, received messages appear on stdout.
For client: stdin is sent to server, received messages appear on stdout.
For cache: stdin accepts cache commands, responses appear on stdout (like redis-cli).
Not supported for proxy. Press Ctrl+C to detach (runtime continues running).
If the runtime is already running, attaches without restarting.
.RE
.TP
.BI "stop " "name|pattern" "..."
Stop one or more running runtimes. Accepts multiple names, glob patterns
.RB ( * ", " ? ", " [] ),
or a mix. With
.BR * ,
stops all running runtimes.
.TP
.BI "remove " "name|pattern" "..."
Remove one or more runtimes from the registry. Cannot be undone.
Accepts multiple names, glob patterns, or a mix. Running runtimes
are stopped first. With
.BR * ,
stops and removes all.
.TP
.BI "send " "name" " [message]"
Send a message to a running server or client runtime.
For servers, the message is broadcast to all connected clients.
For clients, the message is sent to the connected server.
Respects the runtime's mode setting (fails if mode is receive-only).
.PP
If no message is given, reads from stdin:
.nf
echo "hello" | socketley send myserver
cat data.txt | socketley send myclient
.fi
.TP
.BI "stats " "name|pattern" "..."
Display runtime statistics including total connections, messages processed,
bytes in/out, and type-specific counters. Accepts multiple names, glob
patterns, or a mix. With
.BR * ,
shows stats for all runtimes.
.IP
Three of the fields describe how the runtime's I/O is running rather than what
it has done, and all three are read from the ring itself rather than from what
was asked for \(em a kernel can refuse an SQPOLL ring and a cpuset can refuse
the pinning, and a runtime whose real polling mode and CPU set cannot be read
back is one nobody can debug:
.RS
.TP
.B sqpoll
.BR on ,
.BR off ,
or
.B off(refused)
when a poller was asked for and the kernel would not give one.
.TP
.B cpus
The CPU set in force, in the same spelling
.B \-\-cpus
accepts, with
.B (not applied)
appended when the pinning was attempted and refused.
.TP
.B sq_thread_cpu
The CPU the polling kernel thread was pinned to, or
.B \-
when there is no poller or it is unpinned.
.RE
.IP
For a runtime running in its own process these describe that process's ring, not
the daemon's.
.TP
.BI "restart " "name " "[flags]"
Stop and start a runtime in one step. The runtime keeps the
configuration it already has. Equivalent to
.B stop
followed by
.B start
with the same arguments: the extra flags are handed to
.BR start ,
which understands only
.BR \-i ,
so they do not reconfigure anything. To restart with a changed setting,
.B edit
first:
.nf
socketley edit api \-p 9001
socketley restart api
.fi
.TP
.BI "reload " "name|pattern" "..."
Restart one or more running runtimes (stop + start). Accepts multiple
names, glob patterns, or a mix. Non-running runtimes are skipped.
With
.BR * ,
restarts all running runtimes.
.TP
.BI "reload-lua " "name|pattern" "..."
Hot-reload the Lua script attached to one or more runtimes. The Lua
context is destroyed and re-created from the original script path.
Accepts multiple names, glob patterns, or a mix. Runtimes without
Lua scripts are skipped. With
.BR * ,
reloads Lua on all runtimes that have scripts loaded.
.TP
.BI "show " "name|pattern" "..."
Print runtime JSON configuration to stdout. Accepts multiple names,
glob patterns, or a mix. With
.BR * ,
prints all runtimes.
.TP
.BI "edit " "name " "[flags]"
Edit a runtime's configuration. When called with flags (e.g. \-p 9001),
applies the changes directly. The flags accepted here are a subset of the
ones
.B create
takes: the common settings
.RB ( \-p ,
.BR \-b / \-bp / \-bt / \-bpt ,
.BR \-\-log ,
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-\-max\-connections ,
.BR \-\-rate\-limit ,
.BR \-\-global\-rate\-limit ,
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout ,
.BR \-g ),
the server and client flags, and the proxy mesh flags
.RB ( \-\-strategy ,
.BR \-\-health\-* ,
.BR \-\-circuit\-* ,
.BR \-\-backend\-timeout ,
.BR \-\-retry ,
.BR \-\-retry\-all ).
Anything else \(em including
.BR \-\-lua ,
.BR \-\-drain ,
.BR \-\-reconnect ,
the proxy's
.BR \-\-backend ,
.B \-\-protocol
and
.BR \-\-sidecar ,
and every cache flag \(em is refused with
.B "unknown flag"
and exit status 1; change those through the editor or
.BR import ,
which reach the full field set.
The TLS flags are the exception to that wording. Editing is the usual way to
put encryption on something already running, so on
.I every
runtime type
.BR \-\-tls ,
.BR \-\-cert ,
.B \-\-key
and
.B \-\-ca
\(em and, on a proxy,
.BR \-\-client\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.B \-\-client\-key
\(em are accepted here and take effect at the runtime's next start.
Certificates are read when a runtime starts, so a running one keeps the
identity it handshook with until it is restarted. An edit that would leave the
set incomplete \(em a certificate with no
.BR \-\-tls ,
.B \-\-tls
with no key pair on a listener, half a client-certificate pair, or
.B \-\-tls
on a cache that replicates \(em is refused and changes nothing, so a mistyped
flag cannot leave a runtime half-configured. See
.BR LIMITATIONS .
When called without flags (or with only \-r/\-\-reload),
opens the runtime's JSON configuration in
.BR $VISUAL ,
.BR $EDITOR ,
or
.BR vim .
After saving, changes are validated and applied.
.TP
.BI "edit " "name " "\-r"
Interactive editor mode with auto-reload. After saving changes, the
runtime's Lua script is automatically hot-reloaded. Alias:
.BR \-\-reload .
.TP
.BI "import " "name " "json"
Update a runtime's configuration from a JSON string. With no
.I json
argument the document is read from standard input instead, so
.B show
pipes straight into
.BR import :
.nf
socketley show name | socketley import name
.fi
That form has no command-line length limit, which the quoted one does.
Whitespace outside string literals is stripped before the document is sent,
so the pretty-printed output of
.B show
is accepted as-is. Accepts the same
fields as the interactive editor (see below). Fields marked
.B (read-only)
are ignored. Fields marked
.B (requires restart)
cannot be changed while the runtime is running.
Port, protocol, and UDP settings cannot be changed while running.
.PP
A JSON document is held to the same rules the flags are: on every runtime type
.BR tls ,
.BR cert_path ,
.BR key_path " and " ca_path
are accepted as a complete set and refused as an incomplete one. On a
.B cache
a document setting
.B tls
alongside
.B replicate_target
is rejected outright and nothing is applied, because replication is not
encrypted (see
.BR LIMITATIONS ).
.BR client_ca ,
.B client_cert
and
.B client_key
belong to a proxy alone.
A document that simply omits those fields is accepted and clears them, which
is how a runtime restored from an older state file is made startable again:
.nf
socketley show name > cfg.json   # drop the TLS fields in an editor
socketley import name "$(cat cfg.json)"
.fi
.SS Edit: JSON Configuration Fields
The interactive editor opens a JSON file with the following fields.
Fields marked
.B (read-only)
are ignored on import. Fields marked
.B (requires restart)
cannot be changed while the runtime is running \(em stop the runtime first.
.PP
.B Identity fields:
.TS
l l.
name	Runtime name \fB(requires restart)\fR
id	6-char hex identifier (read-only, ignored on import)
type	Runtime type: server, client, proxy, cache (read-only, ignored on import)
was_running	Internal daemon state (ignored on import)
.TE
.PP
.B Common fields (all types):
.TS
l l l.
Field	Type	Description
_
port	number	Listener/connection port \fB(requires restart)\fR
log_file	string	State transition log file path
write_file	string	Message write file path
lua_script	string	Lua script path (absolute)
bash_output	bool	Output messages to stdout
bash_prefix	bool	Prefix messages with [name]
bash_timestamp	bool	Prefix messages with [HH:MM:SS]
max_connections	number	Max concurrent connections (0 = unlimited)
rate_limit	number	Messages/sec per connection (0 = unlimited)
global_rate_limit	number	Messages/sec across all connections (0 = unlimited)
idle_timeout	number	Close idle connections after N seconds (0 = disabled)
drain	bool	Graceful shutdown (flush writes before close)
tls	bool	Terminate TLS on the listener \fB(server, proxy)\fR; dial TLS \fB(client)\fR
cert_path	string	PEM certificate \fB(server, proxy; needs tls)\fR; on a \fBclient\fR, the certificate it presents
key_path	string	PEM private key \fB(server, proxy; needs tls)\fR; on a \fBclient\fR, the key for cert_path
ca_path	string	Client CA for mutual TLS \fB(server, needs tls)\fR; on a \fBproxy\fR, the trust store for TLS backends; on a \fBclient\fR, the trust store for its target
target	string	Client target host:port \fB(requires restart)\fR
cache_name	string	Connected cache runtime name
group	string	Group tag for dynamic proxy discovery
.TE
.PP
.B Server fields:
.TS
l l l.
Field	Type	Description
_
mode	string	"inout", "in", "out", or "master"
udp	bool	Use UDP instead of TCP \fB(requires restart)\fR
master_pw	string	Static password for master mode
master_forward	bool	Forward non-master messages to master
.TE
.PP
.B Client fields:
.TS
l l l.
Field	Type	Description
_
mode	string	"inout", "in", or "out"
udp	bool	Use UDP instead of TCP \fB(requires restart)\fR
reconnect	number	Auto-reconnect max attempts (-1=off, 0=infinite)
.TE
.PP
.B Proxy fields:
.TS
l l l.
Field	Type	Description
_
protocol	string	"http" or "tcp" \fB(requires restart)\fR
strategy	string	"round-robin", "random", or "lua"
backends	array	List of backend addresses ["ip:port", "@group", ...]
health_check	string	"tcp" or "http" (empty = disabled)
health_interval	number	Health check interval in seconds (default 5)
health_path	string	HTTP health check path (default "/health")
health_threshold	number	Failures to mark unhealthy (default 3)
circuit_threshold	number	Errors to open circuit (default 5)
circuit_timeout	number	Seconds before half-open (default 30)
backend_timeout	number	Seconds for the first response byte (default 2, 0 = off)
retry	number	Retry count (0 = disabled)
retry_all	bool	Also retry non-idempotent methods
client_ca	string	CA for certificates clients present \fB(needs tls)\fR
client_cert	string	Client certificate presented to backends
client_key	string	Its private key (both or neither)
sidecar	bool	Enable sidecar defaults
.TE
.PP
.B Cache fields:
.TS
l l l.
Field	Type	Description
_
persistent_path	string	Persistence file path
cache_mode	string	"readonly", "readwrite", or "admin"
resp_forced	bool	Force RESP2 protocol \fB(requires restart)\fR
replicate_target	string	Leader host:port \fB(requires restart)\fR
max_memory	number	Max memory in bytes (0 = unlimited)
eviction	string	"noeviction", "allkeys-lru", "allkeys-random"
.TE
.PP
.B Example JSON (server):
.nf
{
    "name": "api",
    "id": "a1b2c3",
    "type": "server",
    "port": 9000,
    "was_running": true,
    "lua_script": "/home/user/handler.lua",
    "max_connections": 100,
    "rate_limit": 1000,
    "mode": "inout"
}
.fi
.TP
.B dashboard
Print the URL of the built-in web dashboard. Requires
.B config.metrics_port
to be set in the daemon configuration file (see
.BR "Daemon Configuration" ).
The dashboard provides a live-updating view of all runtimes, connections,
and throughput. Example output:
.nf
Dashboard: http://localhost:9100/
.fi
.TP
.B ls \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR | \fB\-j\fR | \fB\-\-format json\fR] [\fB\-\-silent\fR | \fB\-s\fR] [\fIcolumns\fR]
List all runtimes (all states). Docker-style output with columns:
ID, NAME, TYPE, PORT, CONN (connections), OWNED BY, STATUS, CREATED, GROUP.
With \fB\-\-json\fR or \fB\-\-format json\fR, outputs a JSON array of runtime objects.
With \fB\-\-silent\fR or \fB\-s\fR, prints only runtime names (one per line).
.RS
.PP
.B Column selectors
\(em pass one or more to show only those columns:
.BR \-\-id ,
.BR \-\-name ,
.BR \-\-type ,
.BR \-\-port ,
.BR \-\-status ,
.BR \-\-conn ,
.BR \-\-owner ,
.BR \-\-created ,
.BR \-\-group .
.RE
.TP
.B ps \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR | \fB\-j\fR | \fB\-\-format json\fR] [\fB\-\-silent\fR | \fB\-s\fR] [\fIcolumns\fR]
List only running runtimes. Same format as
.BR ls .
With \fB\-\-json\fR, outputs a JSON array (includes uptime field).
With \fB\-\-silent\fR or \fB\-s\fR, prints only runtime names (one per line).
.RS
.PP
.B Column selectors:
.BR \-\-id ,
.BR \-\-name ,
.BR \-\-type ,
.BR \-\-port ,
.BR \-\-uptime ,
.BR \-\-conn ,
.BR \-\-owner ,
.BR \-\-created .
.RE
.TP
.BI "owner " "name"
Show ownership info for a runtime: parent name, children list, and
on_parent_stop policy (stop or remove).
.TP
.BI "cluster " "[subcommand]"
Inspect the cluster. Requires a running daemon with cluster mode enabled
(via
.B daemon \-\-name
+
.BR "daemon \-\-cluster" ).
The cluster directory is read from the daemon automatically.
.RS
.TP
.B ls
List all daemons in the cluster with runtime counts and heartbeat status.
.TP
.B ps
List all runtimes across the entire cluster.
.TP
.BI "group " "name"
Show all members of a specific group across the cluster.
.TP
.BI "show " "daemon\-name"
Print a daemon's raw cluster JSON file.
.TP
.B stats
Show cluster summary: daemon counts, runtime totals, group membership.
.TP
.B watch
Continuously refresh the cluster view every 2 seconds (Ctrl+C to stop).
.RE
.TP
.B version \fR| \fB\-\-version\fR | \fB\-v\fR
Print the socketley version number and exit.
.TP
.BI "\-\-lua " "lua_file"
Load Lua orchestration configuration. Optionally configures daemon name
and cluster mode, then creates multiple runtimes from a single config file.
.SH DAEMON FLAGS
These flags configure the running daemon via IPC commands. The daemon is
auto-started if not already running.
.TP
.BI "\-\-name " "name" " | \-n " "name"
Set this daemon's identity. Required before enabling cluster mode.
Can be changed at any time; if cluster mode is active, the cluster file
is republished under the new name (old name is removed).
.TP
.BI "\-\-cluster " "dir"
Enable cluster mode on the given shared directory. Each daemon publishes
its runtime state there every 2 seconds and scans for peer daemons. Proxy
.B @group
backends automatically discover runtimes on remote daemons.
In Docker Compose, mount a shared volume at this path.
The advertised host address is set automatically via
.BR gethostname (3).
Requires
.B \-\-name
to be set first.
.SH COMMON FLAGS
These flags work with all runtime types:
.TP
.BI "\-p " "port"
Set listener port (server, proxy, cache) or target port (client).
.TP
.B \-s
Start immediately after creation.
.TP
.B \-\-test
Dry run. Validate configuration without starting.
.TP
.BI "\-\-log " "file"
Log state transitions (created, running, stopped, failed) to file.
.TP
.BI "\-w " "file"
Write every received message to file.
.TP
.BI "\-\-lua " "script"
Attach Lua configuration script with callbacks.
.TP
.B \-b
Output received messages to stdout (bash mode).
.TP
.B \-bp
Output messages with runtime name prefix: [name] message
.TP
.B \-bt
Output messages with timestamp: [HH:MM:SS] message
.TP
.B \-bpt
Output messages with both timestamp and prefix: [HH:MM:SS] [name] message
.TP
.BI "\-\-max\-connections " "n"
Limit maximum concurrent connections. New connections beyond this limit
are immediately closed. 0 means unlimited (default). Alias:
.BR \-\-max\-conn .
.TP
.BI "\-\-rate\-limit " "n"
Limit messages per second per connection using a token bucket algorithm.
Excess messages are silently dropped. 0 means unlimited (default).
.TP
.BI "\-\-global\-rate\-limit " "n"
Limit total messages per second across all connections using a shared
token bucket. Excess messages are silently dropped (server) or responded
with an error (cache). 0 means unlimited (default).
.TP
.BI "\-\-idle\-timeout " "seconds"
Close connections that have been idle (no data received) for longer than
the specified number of seconds. A sweep timer runs every 30 seconds to
check for idle connections. 0 means disabled (default).
.TP
.B \-\-drain
Enable graceful shutdown. When stopping, the runtime closes its listener
but flushes all pending write queues before closing connections.
.TP
.BI "\-\-cpus " "set"
The CPUs this runtime's I/O may run on. Accepts
.BR max ,
a range such as
.BR 0\-3 ,
or a list such as
.B 0,1,2,3
whose elements may themselves be ranges
.RB ( 0\-1,3 ).
Default
.BR max ,
which applies no pinning at all \(em not a mask of the CPUs that are online now,
so a CPU that comes online later is still available to the loop.
.IP
A CPU this machine does not have, a descending range, or a set that comes out
empty is refused when the runtime is created. It is never quietly rounded up to
.BR max :
a config written for a sixteen-core machine and deployed on a four-core one
would otherwise run nowhere near where its author believes, and every
measurement taken afterwards would be measuring something else.
.IP
There is deliberately no
.B \-\-rings
to go with this. A ring is a queue between one thread and the kernel, so a count
of rings is only ever a count of I/O threads wearing a disguise, and two rings on
one thread are strictly worse than one \(em more submission state to keep, no
more parallelism. What is left to configure is which CPUs the thread may use.
.TP
.B \-\-sqpoll
.TQ
.B \-\-no\-sqpoll
Whether this runtime's ring carries an io_uring submission-queue polling kernel
thread.
.B \-\-no\-sqpoll
is the default.
.IP
With it, submitting work is a store to a queue tail and costs no system call;
without it, each batch costs one
.BR io_uring_enter (2).
The price is a kernel thread per ring, spinning on that queue for as long as the
ring exists whether or not anything is being submitted \(em worth a core to a
runtime saturating a link, worth nothing to one waiting for its next connection.
.IP
A ring's polling mode and its CPU set are fixed by the kernel when the ring is
created, and a ring belongs to a process, not to a runtime: every runtime inside
one daemon shares that daemon's ring. So these two flags state what a runtime
requires, and
.B create
refuses a requirement the ring it would run on does not meet, naming the
.B socketley daemon
flag or the
.B SOCKETLEY_SQPOLL
/
.B SOCKETLEY_CPUS
setting that would satisfy it. They cannot be changed with
.BR edit .
The values in force are reported by
.BR "socketley stats" .
.IP
Both are persisted with the runtime, and the same refusal is made again when a
persisted runtime is restored: a daemon restarted without the setting that
satisfied a runtime's requirement leaves that runtime
.BR failed ,
with the reason in the daemon log, rather than starting it on a ring that does
not meet it and reporting the ring it got. A runtime that asked for neither flag
records neither and is never refused for one.
.TP
.B \-\-tls
.TQ
.BI "\-\-cert " "file"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-key " "file"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-ca " "file"
.B "All runtime types."
Encrypt this runtime's transport. What that means depends on whether the role
listens or dials.
.PP
On a
.BR server ", " proxy " or " cache ,
TLS terminates on the runtime's own listening port, and
.B \-\-tls
requires both
.BI \-\-cert " file"
(a PEM certificate, chain allowed) and
.BI \-\-key " file"
(its PEM private key) \(em a listener has to have something to terminate TLS
with.
.PP
A
.B cache
terminates TLS exactly as a server does, and everything above the record layer
is unchanged: RESP is still auto-detected from the first byte of the
.I decrypted
stream, so a Redis client speaks the protocol it always did with TLS switched
on, and the line protocol works on the same port. This is what allows a cache
to be reached across a network it does not trust.
.PP
On a
.BR client ,
TLS is dialled rather than terminated, and
.B \-\-tls
on its own is the complete configuration: there is no listening port to hold a
certificate, and the peer is verified against the system trust store by
default.
.BI \-\-cert " file"
and
.BI \-\-key " file"
are optional there and mean the certificate this client
.I presents
when the peer asks for one (mutual TLS); half a pair is refused, because it
would present nothing while the flags said otherwise.
.PP
.BI \-\-ca " file"
means one thing on each role, because only some of them verify a peer they
dialled. On a
.BR server " or " cache
it enables mutual TLS: a client must then present a certificate signed by that
CA, and one that does not is rejected during the handshake. On a
.B proxy
it is the trust store used to verify the certificate a
.B backend
presents \(em the proxy's own listener asks for client certificates with
.BI \-\-client\-ca " file"
instead. On a
.B client
it is the trust store for the peer named by
.BR \-t ;
without it the system store is used.
See
.B "mTLS Flags"
under
.BR "PROXY FLAGS" .
.PP
A dialled connection \(em a client's target, a proxy's backend \(em is verified
on two counts, and neither can be turned off. The chain must be signed by
something in the trust store, and the certificate must be valid for the host
that was dialled: the name in
.B \-t
for a client, matched against the certificate's DNS SAN, or against its IP SAN
for an address literal. The name checked is always the one configured, never
one read back out of the certificate that arrived. TLS 1.2 is the minimum
version. There is deliberately no skip-verify option: a TLS client that does
not verify buys encryption against an eavesdropper and nothing at all against
somebody in the path, while reporting itself as encrypted either way.
.PP
Certificates and trust stores are read when the runtime starts, so
.B reload
is how a renewed certificate is installed \(em on every role that has one. A
certificate that cannot be loaded, a key that does not match it, and any
incomplete combination (\c
.B \-\-tls
without a key pair on a listener, a key pair or a trust store without
.BR \-\-tls ,
half a client-certificate pair) are refused: the runtime goes to the
.B failed
state and nothing binds the port or dials the target. There is no fallback to
cleartext on any path. A client whose peer fails verification drops the socket
and re-dials on its usual backoff; it never retries in the clear.
.PP
These flags are refused on a
.B client
or a
.B server
combined with
.B \-\-udp
(that would need DTLS), and on a server combined with
.B \-\-upstream
(the upstream leg would still dial in cleartext). On a
.B cache
they are refused together with
.BI \-\-replicate " host:port"
\(em replication is not encrypted, so the pair would stream the whole dataset
in the clear off a port that reports itself as encrypted. See
.B TLS
under
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.TP
.BI "\-g " "name"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-group " "name"
Assign the runtime to a named group. Groups enable dynamic proxy backend
discovery: a proxy using
.B @groupname
as a backend will route to all running members of that group at connection
time. New group members are discovered automatically without restarting
the proxy.
.SH SERVER FLAGS
.TP
.BI "\-\-mode " "mode"
Message flow direction:
.B inout
(bidirectional, default),
.B in
(receive only),
.B out
(send only),
.B master
(one client claims master, only master's messages are broadcast).
.TP
.B \-\-udp
Use UDP instead of TCP. The server binds a single SOCK_DGRAM socket.
Peers are auto-registered on first datagram. Each datagram is one message
(no line-parsing). Broadcast sends to all known peers via sendto().
Not available for cache or proxy. Incompatible with master mode.
.TP
.BI "\-\-master\-pw " "password"
Static password for master mode authentication. A client sends "master <password>"
to claim master role. Only one master at a time; master is released on disconnect.
Alternatively, use the
.B on_master_auth(client_id, password)
Lua callback for dynamic authentication.
.TP
.B \-\-master\-forward
When set in master mode, non-master client messages are forwarded to the master
as "[<fd>] <message>\\n" instead of being silently dropped.
.TP
.BI "\-\-http " "directory"
Serve static files from the given directory over HTTP. When a browser makes a
plain HTTP GET request (without a WebSocket upgrade header), the server responds
with the requested file. GET / serves index.html. HTML files (.html, .htm)
automatically get a WebSocket connection script injected before </head>.
Content-Type is detected from the file extension. WebSocket upgrades continue
to work as before on the same port. Only GET requests are served; other HTTP
methods fall through to raw TCP handling.
.TP
.B \-\-http\-cache
When used with \-\-http, reads all files into memory at startup instead of
reading from disk on each request. Faster for production. The cache is rebuilt
on
.BR reload\-lua .
Without this flag, files are read from disk on each request (suitable for
development).
.TP
.TP
.BI "\-u " "host:port"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-upstream " "host:port"
Add an outbound upstream connection. The server connects to the specified
host:port on startup and auto-reconnects with exponential backoff (1s\(en30s)
if the connection drops. Multiple \-u flags may be specified, or multiple
addresses separated by semicolons (e.g. \-u "feed:9000;cache:6379").
Each upstream gets a stable 1-based conn_id for use in Lua callbacks.
.PP
.RS
Upstream Lua callbacks:
.TP
.B on_upstream(conn_id, data)
Called for each newline-delimited message received from an upstream.
.TP
.B on_upstream_connect(conn_id)
Called when an upstream connects (including reconnects).
.TP
.B on_upstream_disconnect(conn_id)
Called when an upstream disconnects.
.RE
.RS
Upstream Lua actions:
.TP
.B self.upstream_send(conn_id, msg)
Send a message to a specific upstream.
.TP
.B self.upstream_broadcast(msg)
Send a message to all connected upstreams.
.TP
.B self.upstreams()
Returns a table of currently connected upstream conn_ids.
.RE
.TP
.BI "\-\-cache " "name"
Link this server to an existing cache runtime. Clients can then send
"cache <command>" messages (e.g. "cache set foo bar", "cache get foo")
which are executed against the linked cache and the response returned to
the sender only. Normal messages are still processed according to the
server's mode.
.IP
The command is not executed on the connection that asked for it. The
server hands it to the daemon and answers when the reply comes back, one
turn of the event loop later, so a
.B cache
command costs a round trip that the same command typed at the cache's own
port does not. See
.B Cache Access via Server
below for what that guarantees and what it costs.
.SS Multi-Protocol Auto-Detection
Server connections automatically detect the protocol from the first bytes
received. No flags are needed \(em all four protocols coexist on the same
port:
.TP
.B TCP (newline-delimited text)
Default. Lines terminated by
.B \\en
are delivered as messages.
.TP
.B HTTP
Detected when the first bytes are a valid HTTP method (GET, POST, etc.).
Requests are handled by the
.B on_http_request
Lua callback if defined, or by the static file server if
.B \-\-http
is set, or responded with 404 otherwise. HTTP/1.1 keep-alive is supported.
.TP
.B WebSocket
Detected when an HTTP request contains
.BR "Upgrade: websocket" .
The server completes the RFC 6455 handshake and transparently frames/deframes
messages. The
.B on_websocket
callback fires after the upgrade.
.TP
.B RESP (Redis protocol)
Detected when the first byte is
.BR * .
Useful for cache-linked servers or custom RESP-based protocols.
.PP
Browser example:
.nf
const ws = new WebSocket("ws://localhost:9000");
ws.onmessage = e => console.log(e.data);
ws.send("hello");
.fi
.SS Cache Access via Server
When a server is linked to a cache with
.BR \-\-cache ,
clients can send "cache <command>" messages to execute commands directly against
the linked cache. The response is sent back to the sender only (never broadcast).
Normal messages are still processed according to the server's mode.
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache store -p 9001 -s
socketley create server api -p 9000 --cache store -s
# Client sends: "cache set foo bar" → receives "ok"
# Client sends: "cache get foo" → receives "bar"
.fi
.PP
The request is asynchronous. The server does not run the cache's command
interpreter itself \(em it posts the command and answers the client when
the reply arrives, which is the next turn of the daemon's event loop.
That is a round trip the synchronous version did not pay, on the order of
tens of microseconds on loopback; it is what allows the cache to be a
different process later without anything on the client's side changing.
Nothing else waits on it: the server keeps serving every other connection
while the reply is outstanding.
.PP
What holds:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Replies on one connection come back in request order, so a client may
pipeline
.B cache
commands and match answers to questions positionally.
.IP \(bu 2
A command addressed to a cache that is not running is answered with an
.B error:
line rather than with silence.
.IP \(bu 2
A client that shuts down its write side after asking still receives its
answer: the connection is held open until every reply it is owed has been
written.
.RE
.PP
What does not: nothing is promised about the order of a
.B cache
reply against other traffic on the same connection. A broadcast goes to
every client except the sender and a cache reply goes to the sender
alone, so there is no interleaving to define.
.PP
A connection may have at most 4096 unanswered
.B cache
commands outstanding, and the daemon at most 32768 across all of them. A
client that pipelines past its share is disconnected rather than refused
in place: a refusal written now would arrive ahead of the answers already
queued in front of it, and a client that cannot tell which answer belongs
to which question has no protocol left.
.PP
Default port: 8000
.SH CLIENT FLAGS
.TP
.BI "\-t " "host:port"
Target server to connect to. Default: 127.0.0.1:8000
.TP
.BI "\-\-mode " "mode"
Message flow direction:
.B inout
(bidirectional, default),
.B in
(receive only),
.B out
(send only).
.TP
.B \-\-udp
Use UDP instead of TCP. Creates a connected SOCK_DGRAM socket.
Each datagram is one message (no line-parsing).
Not available for cache or proxy.
.TP
.BI "\-\-reconnect " "[max\-attempts]"
Enable automatic reconnection with exponential backoff when the connection
is lost. Backoff starts at 1 second and doubles each attempt up to 30 seconds,
with random jitter. Set to 0 for infinite attempts (default when flag is given
without a value). Set to a positive number to limit reconnection attempts.
.PP
A client also takes the TLS flags \(em see
.B \-\-tls
under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" .
.B \-\-tls
alone dials the target over TLS and verifies it against the system trust
store;
.BI \-\-ca " file"
supplies a trust store instead, and
.BI \-\-cert " file"
with
.BI \-\-key " file"
present a certificate to a peer that asks for one. The host in
.B \-t
is the name the certificate has to be valid for. A verification failure costs
the socket and the client re-dials on the backoff above; it is never retried
in cleartext, and there is no way to skip the check.
.B \-\-tls
with
.B \-\-udp
is refused \(em that would need DTLS.
.SH PROXY FLAGS
.TP
.BI "\-\-backend " "addr"
Add backend server. Can be ip:port, runtime name, or
.BI @ groupname
to dynamically target all running members of that group. Repeatable or comma-separated.
Group backends are resolved at connection time, so new group members are picked up
automatically without restarting the proxy.
.IP
An address may carry a scheme:
.BI https:// host : port
is reached over TLS,
.BI http:// host : port
in cleartext, and a bare
.IB host : port
follows whatever the backend-TLS options below say. The port may be omitted
after a scheme, in which case it is 443 or 80. See
.B "mTLS Flags"
for what verifies such a connection.
.TP
.BI "\-\-strategy " "strategy"
Load balancing strategy:
.B round-robin
(default),
.B random
,
.B lua
(custom via on_route callback).
.TP
.BI "\-\-protocol " "protocol"
.B http
(default, path-based routing) or
.B tcp
(raw byte forwarding).
.PP
Default port: 8080
.PP
HTTP mode: Requests to /<proxy-name>/* are forwarded with prefix stripped.
.SS Health Check Flags
.TP
.BI "\-\-health\-check " "[tcp|http]"
Enable active health checks for backends. In
.B tcp
mode, the proxy opens a TCP connection to each backend at the configured
interval \(em success means the port is listening. In
.B http
mode, the proxy sends an HTTP GET to
.B \-\-health\-path
and considers the backend healthy on a 2xx response.
Unhealthy backends are removed from the load balancer pool until they
recover.
.TP
.BI "\-\-health\-interval " "seconds"
Interval between health checks in seconds. Default: 5.
.TP
.BI "\-\-health\-path " "path"
HTTP health check path. Default:
.BR /health .
Only used when
.B \-\-health\-check
is set to
.BR http .
.TP
.BI "\-\-health\-threshold " "n"
Number of consecutive health check failures before a backend is marked
unhealthy. Default: 3. A single successful check restores the backend.
.SS Circuit Breaker Flags
.TP
.BI "\-\-circuit\-threshold " "n"
Number of consecutive backend errors (connection refused, timeout, 5xx)
that trips the circuit breaker to the
.B open
state. While open, all requests to that backend fail immediately without
attempting a connection. Default: 5.
.TP
.BI "\-\-circuit\-timeout " "seconds"
Seconds before an open circuit transitions to
.BR half-open .
In half-open state, a single probe request is allowed through \(em if it
succeeds, the circuit closes (fully healthy); if it fails, the circuit
re-opens. Default: 30.
.TP
.BI "\-\-backend\-timeout " "seconds"
How long a backend may take to produce the
.I first
byte of a response before the request is given up on. The request is
then retried on another backend if
.B \-\-retry
allows it, and answered
.B 504 Gateway Timeout
otherwise; either way the backend is charged with an error, so the
circuit breaker above opens and takes it out of rotation. Default: 2.
Set to 0 to wait indefinitely.
.IP
This is the only thing that notices a backend which accepts connections
and then stops answering. Such a backend passes a TCP health check, and
a request that never completes is recorded as neither success nor
failure, so without a deadline it keeps its full share of the rotation
and every request routed to it is held until the client gives up. HTTP
mode only \(em TCP mode has no request/response framing to measure. Only
the wait for the first byte is bounded, so a slow or streaming response
body is never cut short once the backend has begun answering.
.IP
The 2-second default applies whether or not the flag is given. If any
endpoint behind this proxy normally needs longer than that to
.I begin
answering \(em a report that is computed on demand, a cold cache, a long
query \(em raise
.B \-\-backend\-timeout
to cover it or set it to
.B 0
to disable the deadline. Left at the default, such an endpoint has its
requests cut off at 2 seconds and is charged an error each time, which
opens the circuit breaker on a backend that is working correctly.
.SS Retry Flags
.TP
.BI "\-\-retry " "n"
Retry failed requests up to
.I n
times on the next backend in the pool (0 = disabled, default). Only
idempotent HTTP methods (GET, HEAD, PUT, DELETE, OPTIONS) are retried
unless
.B \-\-retry\-all
is set. For TCP mode, any failed connection attempt is retried.
.TP
.B \-\-retry\-all
Also retry non-idempotent methods (POST, PATCH). Use with caution \(em
the request body is replayed to the next backend, which may cause
duplicate side effects.
.SS Process Isolation
.TP
.B \-\-out\-of\-process
Run this proxy in a worker process supervised by the daemon, instead of
inside the daemon itself. Off by default.
.IP
The daemon forks and execs itself, hands the worker its configuration and
its routing table over a control socket, and keeps a placeholder for it in
the runtime map. What the CLI shows is unchanged:
.BR ps ,
.BR ls ,
.BR inspect ,
.B stats
and the Prometheus endpoint report the same figures they do for an
in-process proxy, because the worker publishes them and the daemon stores
them where an in-process runtime would have written them.
.IP
The port is bound in the worker; no descriptor is passed across the
boundary. If the worker dies the daemon restarts it and the runtime stays
.BR running ;
if it dies more than five times in ten seconds the runtime is left
.B failed
rather than being forked in a loop.
.IP
Settable only at
.BR create .
.B edit
rejects it, because honouring it would mean moving a configured runtime
between address spaces underneath whatever already points at it.
.IP
Lua callbacks run inside the worker, against the worker's own state; a
script that reaches for other runtimes through the management API does not
find them there. See
.B "Out-of-process proxies"
under
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.SS mTLS Flags
A proxy has two legs and each has its own half of mutual TLS. The names say
which: \fBclient\fR here always means "a TLS client", and the proxy is one of
those towards its backends while its own callers are ones towards it.
.TP
.BI "\-\-client\-ca " "path"
The CA that a certificate presented
.I by a client
must be signed by. This is mutual TLS on the proxy's own listening port, so it
requires
.BR \-\-tls " " \-\-cert " " \-\-key ;
without them it is refused, because on a cleartext port it would authenticate
nobody while
.B inspect
reported mutual TLS.
.TP
.BI "\-\-client\-cert " "path"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-client\-key " "path"
The certificate and key this proxy presents
.I to a backend
that asks for one. Both or neither: half a pair loads nothing, so it is
refused rather than leaving a leg that presents no certificate after the flag
said it would.
.PP
Any of
.BR \-\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.B \-\-client\-key
also turns the backend leg itself to TLS, for every backend \(em none of the
three means anything against a cleartext peer, and honouring them without
encrypting would be the fail-open reading of all three. Use an
.B https://
backend address instead to encrypt one backend and leave its neighbours alone.
.PP
A TLS backend connection is
.B verified,
always: the chain against
.BI \-\-ca " file"
when one is given and against the system CA store otherwise, and the
certificate against the endpoint the request was routed to \(em a host name
matched against the certificate's DNS names with partial wildcards refused, an
address literal against its IP SAN. TLS 1.2 is the minimum version. There is
no option to disable any of that; a backend presenting a private-CA or
self-signed certificate needs that CA in
.BI \-\-ca " file"
or in the store OpenSSL reads. A backend whose certificate fails either check
is answered 502 (or retried, where
.B \-\-retry
is set) and is charged to its circuit breaker \(em the request is never sent
in the clear as a fallback.
.PP
Two costs come with the backend leg. Connections to a TLS backend are
.B not pooled,
so each request pays for a handshake: a pooled socket is handed out on a host
and a port, which cannot express the session and peer identity a TLS
connection carries. And
.B \-\-health\-check http
is refused alongside it, because a plaintext probe against a TLS port would
mark every backend unhealthy; use
.BR "\-\-health\-check tcp" .
.SS Sidecar Mode
.TP
.B \-\-sidecar
Convenience shorthand that enables a service-mesh-ready configuration
in a single flag. Equivalent to:
.nf
\-\-health\-check tcp \-\-circuit\-threshold 5 \-\-circuit\-timeout 30 \-\-retry 2 \-\-drain
.fi
.IP
Individual flags can still be specified alongside
.B \-\-sidecar
to override the defaults (e.g.
.BR "\-\-sidecar \-\-health\-check http \-\-health\-path /ready" ).
.IP
.B \-\-sidecar
does not set
.BR \-\-backend\-timeout ,
but it runs into it: the deadline is 2 seconds by default, and the
circuit breaker
.B \-\-sidecar
turns on is what the expiry is charged to. Five slow requests in a row
open the circuit for 30 seconds. Add
.BI "\-\-backend\-timeout " seconds
alongside
.B \-\-sidecar
whenever the application behind it can take longer than 2 seconds to
send its first response byte, or
.B \-\-backend\-timeout 0
to switch the deadline off.
.PP
Example:
.nf
socketley create proxy myapp \-\-sidecar \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 \\
    -p 8080 \-\-health\-check tcp -s

socketley create proxy slowapp \-\-sidecar \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 \\
    -p 8080 \-\-backend\-timeout 30 -s
.fi
.SH CACHE FLAGS
.TP
.BI "\-\-persistent " "file"
Persistent storage file. Cache is saved on stop and loaded on start.
.TP
.BI "\-\-mode " "mode"
Access control mode:
.B readonly
(GET, SIZE only),
.B readwrite
(GET, SET, DEL, SIZE \- default),
.B admin
(all commands including FLUSH, LOAD).
.TP
.BI "\-\-maxmemory " "size"
Maximum memory for cache data. Supports K, M, G suffixes (e.g., 100M, 1G).
When exceeded, behavior depends on eviction policy. 0 means unlimited (default).
.TP
.BI "\-\-eviction " "policy"
Eviction policy when maxmemory is reached:
.B noeviction
(reject writes, default),
.B allkeys\-lru
(evict least recently used),
.B allkeys\-random
(evict random keys).
.TP
.B \-\-resp
Force RESP2 protocol mode for all connections (Redis wire protocol).
By default, the cache auto-detects RESP mode when a connection's first
byte is '*'. With this flag, all connections use RESP regardless.
.TP
.BI "\-\-replicate " "host:port"
Connect as a follower to the specified leader cache. The follower receives
a full data dump on connect, then receives ongoing write command replication.
The follower operates in read-only mode.
.IP
Replication is
.I not
encrypted, in either direction, so it cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-tls :
that pair is refused at
.BR create ,
by
.BR edit " and " import ,
and again at every
.BR start .
A
.B \-\-tls
cache also refuses the
.B replicate
command, so it never becomes a leader either. See
.B TLS
under
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.TP
.B \-\-tls
.TQ
.BI "\-\-cert " "file"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-key " "file"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-ca " "file"
Terminate TLS on this cache's listening port, exactly as a server runtime does
on its own.
.B \-\-tls
requires
.BI \-\-cert " file"
and
.BI \-\-key " file";
.BI \-\-ca " file"
additionally demands a certificate from every connecting client (mutual TLS).
RESP and the line protocol both run on the decrypted stream, so an existing
Redis client needs no change beyond speaking TLS. Described in full under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" .
.PP
Default port: 9000
.SS Cache Protocol
Connect via TCP and send commands (newline-terminated, all lowercase).
.PP
.B Strings:
.TP
.BI "set " "key value"
Store a key-value pair. Returns "ok", "error: type conflict", or
"error: out of memory".
.TP
.BI "get " "key"
Retrieve value. Returns value or "nil".
.TP
.BI "del " "key"
Delete key (any type). Returns "ok" or "nil".
.TP
.BI "exists " "key"
Check if key exists. Returns "1" or "0".
.TP
.BI "incr " "key"
Increment integer value by 1. Returns new integer.
.TP
.BI "decr " "key"
Decrement integer value by 1. Returns new integer.
.TP
.BI "incrby " "key delta"
Increment integer value by delta. Returns new integer.
.TP
.BI "decrby " "key delta"
Decrement integer value by delta. Returns new integer.
.TP
.BI "append " "key value"
Append to string value. Returns new length.
.TP
.BI "strlen " "key"
Return string length. Returns 0 if missing.
.TP
.BI "getset " "key newvalue"
Set new value and return old value. Returns old value or "nil".
.TP
.BI "mget " "key [key ...]"
Get multiple keys. Returns one value per line (nil if missing), then "end".
.TP
.BI "mset " "key value [key value ...]"
Set multiple key-value pairs atomically. Returns "ok".
.TP
.BI "type " "key"
Return type of key: "string", "list", "set", "hash", or "none".
.TP
.BI "keys " "pattern"
List all matching keys (glob patterns, "*" for all). Returns keys then "end".
.TP
.BI "setnx " "key value"
Set key only if it does not exist. Returns "1" (set) or "0" (not set).
.TP
.BI "setex " "key seconds value"
Set key with expiry. Equivalent to SET + EXPIRE. Returns "ok".
.TP
.BI "psetex " "key milliseconds value"
Set key with millisecond-precision expiry. Returns "ok".
.TP
.BI "scan " "cursor [match pattern] [count n]"
Cursor-based key iteration. Returns next cursor (0 = done), then matching keys, then "end".
Use cursor=0 to start; repeat with returned cursor until 0 is returned.
.TP
.B size
Return number of stored keys (all types).
.PP
.B Lists (ordered, deque-backed):
.TP
.BI "lpush " "key value"
Insert at front. Returns "ok" or "error: type conflict".
.TP
.BI "rpush " "key value"
Insert at back.
.TP
.BI "lpop " "key"
Remove and return front element. Returns value or "nil".
.TP
.BI "rpop " "key"
Remove and return back element.
.TP
.BI "llen " "key"
Return list length.
.TP
.BI "lindex " "key index"
Return element at index (negative = from end).
.TP
.BI "lrange " "key start stop"
Return range of elements (multi-line, "end" terminator). Negative indices supported.
.PP
.B Sets (unique unordered members):
.TP
.BI "sadd " "key member"
Add member. Returns "ok", "exists", or "error: type conflict".
.TP
.BI "srem " "key member"
Remove member. Returns "ok" or "nil".
.TP
.BI "sismember " "key member"
Check membership. Returns "1" or "0".
.TP
.BI "scard " "key"
Return set cardinality.
.TP
.BI "smembers " "key"
Return all members (multi-line, "end" terminator).
.PP
.B Hashes (nested key-value maps):
.TP
.BI "hset " "key field value"
Set hash field. Returns "ok", "error: type conflict", or
"error: out of memory".
.TP
.BI "hget " "key field"
Get hash field value. Returns value or "nil".
.TP
.BI "hdel " "key field"
Delete hash field. Returns "ok" or "nil".
.TP
.BI "hlen " "key"
Return number of hash fields.
.TP
.BI "hgetall " "key"
Return all fields and values (multi-line "field value\n", "end" terminator).
.PP
.B TTL / Expiry:
.TP
.BI "expire " "key seconds"
Set time-to-live. Returns "ok" or "nil" (key not found).
.TP
.BI "ttl " "key"
Return remaining seconds, -1 (no ttl), or -2 (not found).
.TP
.BI "persist " "key"
Remove expiry. Returns "ok" or "nil".
.TP
.BI "pexpire " "key milliseconds"
Set millisecond-precision TTL. Returns "1" (set) or "0" (key not found).
.TP
.BI "pttl " "key"
Return remaining TTL in milliseconds, -1 (no ttl), or -2 (not found).
.TP
.BI "expireat " "key unix_seconds"
Set expiry to an absolute Unix timestamp (seconds). Returns "1" or "0".
.TP
.BI "pexpireat " "key unix_ms"
Set expiry to an absolute Unix timestamp in milliseconds. Returns "1" or "0".
.PP
.B Pub/Sub:
.TP
.BI "subscribe " "channel"
Subscribe to a channel. Returns "ok". Messages are delivered as
"message <channel> <payload>\\n".
.TP
.BI "unsubscribe " "channel"
Unsubscribe from a channel. Returns "ok".
.TP
.BI "publish " "channel message"
Publish a message to all subscribers. Returns number of subscribers reached.
.PP
.B Memory Management:
.TP
.BI "maxmemory " ""
Query current maxmemory setting. Returns bytes or "0" (unlimited).
.TP
.BI "memory " ""
Query current memory usage in bytes.
.PP
.B Admin (requires --mode admin):
.TP
.BI "flush " "[path]"
Save cache to file (uses --persistent path if omitted).
.TP
.BI "load " "[path]"
Load cache from file.
.PP
Type enforcement: a key can only hold one data type. Writing to a key of a different type returns "error: type conflict".
Under \fB--maxmemory\fR a write that does not fit is refused with "error: out of memory" (RESP: "OOM command not allowed when used memory > 'maxmemory'"), and the key it would have written is left exactly as it was.
That includes a write over a key that already exists: overwriting a value, appending to it or replacing a hash field is checked against the limit like any other write.
Multi-line responses (lrange, smembers, hgetall) terminate with "end\n".
.SS RESP Protocol (Redis Compatibility)
The cache supports the RESP2 wire protocol for compatibility with Redis clients
(redis-cli, Redis client libraries). RESP mode is auto-detected when a
connection's first byte is '*', or can be forced with
.BR \-\-resp .
.PP
Supported Redis commands (case-insensitive): SET (with EX, PX, NX, XX options),
GET, DEL, EXISTS, PING, DBSIZE, INCR, DECR, INCRBY, DECRBY, APPEND, STRLEN,
GETSET, MGET, MSET, TYPE, KEYS, SETNX, SETEX, PSETEX, SCAN,
LPUSH, RPUSH, LPOP, RPOP, LLEN, LRANGE, LINDEX,
SADD, SREM, SISMEMBER, SCARD, SMEMBERS,
HSET, HGET, HDEL, HLEN, HGETALL,
EXPIRE, TTL, PERSIST, PEXPIRE, PTTL, EXPIREAT, PEXPIREAT,
SUBSCRIBE, UNSUBSCRIBE, PUBLISH.
.PP
Example with redis-cli:
.nf
redis-cli -p 9000 SET mykey myval
redis-cli -p 9000 GET mykey
redis-cli -p 9000 SUBSCRIBE news
.fi
.SS Database Backend
Cache runtimes support four Lua callbacks for DB-backed operation.
Attach a Lua script with
.BR \-\-lua
and implement whichever callbacks you need using any Lua DB library
(luasql-mysql, luasql-postgres, lsqlite3, etc.).
.PP
.TS
tab(|);
l l l.
Callback|Signature|When called
_
on_miss(key)|→ value [, ttl_seconds]|GET returns nil — fetch from DB
on_write(key, value, ttl)|→ nothing|After successful SET/SETEX/SETNX/MSET
on_delete(key)|→ nothing|After DEL — delete from DB
on_expire(key)|→ nothing|After TTL sweep removes key
.TE
.PP
\fBon_miss\fR may return two values (Lua multi-return): the fetched string and an
optional TTL in seconds.
If the value is nil or empty, the GET returns nil to the client normally.
If a value is returned, it is inserted into the cache and returned to
the client transparently (read-through / lazy population).
.PP
\fBon_write\fR is called synchronously on the event loop thread.
For write-heavy loads, use the write-behind pattern: buffer writes in Lua
inside \fBon_write\fR and flush to the DB in \fBon_tick\fR every few seconds.
This decouples SET latency from DB latency entirely.
.PP
Example (SQLite write-behind):
.nf
tick_ms = 2000
local db, pending = nil, {}

function on_start()
    local sq = require("lsqlite3")
    db = sq.open("/tmp/cache.db")
    db:exec("CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS cache_kv (key TEXT PRIMARY KEY, value TEXT)")
end

function on_miss(key)
    for row in db:nrows("SELECT value FROM cache_kv WHERE key=" .. db:quote(key)) do
        return row.value, 300  -- value + 300 s TTL
    end
end

function on_write(key, value, ttl)
    pending[key] = value  -- buffer
end

function on_tick(dt)
    for k, v in pairs(pending) do
        db:exec("INSERT OR REPLACE INTO cache_kv(key,value) VALUES("..db:quote(k)..","..db:quote(v)..")")
    end
    pending = {}
end

function on_delete(key)
    db:exec("DELETE FROM cache_kv WHERE key="..db:quote(key))
end
.fi
.PP
See
.B examples/cache/db-backend.lua
for a full example with MySQL, Postgres, and SQLite backends.
.SS Replication
Cache runtimes support leader-follower replication.
.PP
The follower connects to the leader and receives a full data dump, then
ongoing write commands are forwarded in real-time. Followers are read-only.
.PP
.nf
# Start leader cache
socketley create cache leader -p 9000 -s

# Start follower
socketley create cache follower -p 9001 --replicate 127.0.0.1:9000 -s

# Writes on leader are replicated to follower
echo "set key val" | nc localhost 9000
echo "get key" | nc localhost 9001    # Returns "val"
.fi
.PP
Example with netcat:
.nf
echo "set user:1 alice" | nc localhost 9000
echo "get user:1" | nc localhost 9000
echo "lpush queue task1" | nc localhost 9000
echo "hset user:1 name alice" | nc localhost 9000
echo "expire user:1 300" | nc localhost 9000
echo "subscribe news" | nc localhost 9000
.fi
.SH LUA CONFIGURATION
Lua scripts can define multiple runtimes and attach callbacks.
.PP
.B Module loading:
The Lua
.B require()
function resolves modules relative to the script's directory.
For a script at
.IR /home/user/handler.lua ,
.B require("utils")
loads
.IR /home/user/utils.lua .
The default Lua package path is appended, so standard modules still work.
.SS Configuration File Format
.nf
\-\- Optional: configure daemon name and cluster directory
daemon = {
    name = "node1",
    cluster = "/shared/cluster"
}

runtimes = {
    { type = "server", name = "api", port = 9000, group = "api", start = true },
    { type = "client", name = "cli", port = 9000, target = "127.0.0.1:9000" },
    { type = "proxy",  name = "gw", port = 8080,
      backends = {"@api", "127.0.0.1:9000"}, strategy = "round-robin" },
    { type = "cache",  name = "store", port = 9001,
      persistent = "/data/store.bin" }
}
.fi
.PP
The
.B daemon
table is processed first (name, then cluster). If omitted, the daemon
runs without cluster mode.
.SS Available Callbacks
.TP
.B on_start()
Called when runtime starts.
.TP
.B on_stop()
Called when runtime stops.
.TP
.B on_message(msg)
Called when message received (server/client).
When defined on a server, this callback claims message handling: the default broadcast
(in \fBinout\fR and \fBmaster\fR modes) is suppressed. Use \fBself.broadcast(msg)\fR
to relay messages manually.
.TP
.B on_client_message(client_id, msg)
Called when message received with sender identification (server only).
Fires alongside on_message for backward compatibility.
.TP
.B on_connect(client_id)
Called when a client connects (server/client). Also called on a sub\-server when
a connection is routed to it. The client_id is opaque: for this runtime's own
clients it is the file descriptor, and for a connection routed here from another
runtime it is a handle above 16777216 that names the connection wherever it
lives. Pass it back to
.BR self.send() ,
.BR self.owner_send() ,
or anything else taking a client_id; do not compare it against a descriptor or
assume anything about its value.
.TP
.B on_disconnect(client_id)
Called when a client disconnects (server/client).
.TP
.B on_send(msg)
Called when message sent.
.TP
.B on_route(method, path)
Proxy only. Custom backend selection. Returns backend index (0-based).
Answers only "where does this go"; to accept or refuse a request before any
backend is chosen, see
.BR "Proxy Filter Chain" .
.TP
.B filters
Proxy only. Not a callback but an array of them: see
.BR "Proxy Filter Chain" .
.TP
.B on_proxy_request(client_id, data) \(-> string | nil
Proxy only. Called for every chunk forwarded from a client to a backend.
Return a modified string to replace the data, or
.B nil
to drop the chunk entirely (the backend never receives it).
.TP
.B on_proxy_response(client_id, data) \(-> string | nil
Proxy only. Called for every chunk forwarded from a backend to a client.
Same semantics as
.BR on_proxy_request :
return a replacement string or nil to drop.
.TP
.B on_master_auth(client_id, password)
Server only (master mode). Custom authentication. Return true to grant
master role, false to deny. If defined, overrides
.BR \-\-master\-pw .
.TP
.B on_auth(client_id) \(-> bool
Server only. Called immediately after a client is accepted, before
.B on_connect
fires. Return
.B true
to admit the client or
.B false
to reject and close the connection. If the callback throws a Lua error, the client is also rejected (fail-closed). When no
.B on_auth
callback is defined, all clients are admitted.
.TP
.B on_websocket(client_id, headers)
Server only. Called immediately after a WebSocket upgrade handshake completes,
after
.B on_connect
has already fired. The
.I headers
argument is a Lua table containing any subset of the following keys from the
HTTP upgrade request:
.BR cookie ,
.BR origin ,
.BR protocol
(Sec-WebSocket-Protocol), and
.BR authorization .
Only keys present in the request are set; absent headers are nil.
This callback never fires for plain TCP clients.
.TP
.B on_http_request(req) \(-> table
Server only. Called when an HTTP request is received (auto-detected from the
first bytes; see
.BR "Multi-Protocol Auto-Detection" ).
The
.I req
argument is a table with the following fields:
.nf
method      string   HTTP method ("GET", "POST", etc.)
path        string   Request path (e.g. "/api/users")
query       string   Query string without leading ? (empty if none)
body        string   Request body (empty for GET)
client_id   number   File descriptor of the client
version     string   HTTP version (e.g. "1.1")
headers     table    Request headers {["Host"]="...", ...}
.fi
.IP
Return a table with the HTTP response:
.nf
{
    status  = 200,                              -- HTTP status code
    headers = {["Content-Type"] = "text/html"}, -- response headers
    body    = "<h1>Hello</h1>"                  -- response body
}
.fi
.IP
If no
.B on_http_request
callback is defined, the server falls back to static file serving
.RB ( \-\-http )
or returns 404. Supports HTTP/1.1 keep-alive \(em the connection is reused
for subsequent requests.
.IP
Example:
.nf
function on_http_request(req)
    if req.method == "GET" and req.path == "/health" then
        return {status = 200, body = "ok"}
    end
    return {
        status  = 200,
        headers = {["Content-Type"] = "application/json"},
        body    = '{"method": "' .. req.method .. '", "path": "' .. req.path .. '"}'
    }
end
.fi
.TP
.B on_tick(dt)
Called at a configurable interval while the runtime is running.
.I dt
is the actual elapsed time in milliseconds since the last tick (double).
Requires a top-level
.B tick_ms
global (integer, minimum 10, default 100 if omitted) to be set in the Lua script.
Example:
.nf
tick_ms = 50
function on_tick(dt)
    self.broadcast("ping")
end
.fi
.TP
.B on_upstream(conn_id, data)
Server only. Called for each newline-delimited message received from an
upstream connection. Use \-u to configure upstreams.
.TP
.B on_upstream_connect(conn_id)
Server only. Called when an upstream connects (including auto-reconnects).
.TP
.B on_upstream_disconnect(conn_id)
Server only. Called when an upstream disconnects.
.TP
.B on_cluster_join(daemon)
Cluster only. Called when a new remote daemon is discovered. See
.BR "Cluster Event Callbacks" .
.TP
.B on_cluster_leave(daemon)
Cluster only. Called when a remote daemon becomes stale or disappears.
.TP
.B on_group_change(group, members)
Cluster only. Called when the member count of a group changes.
.SS Proxy Filter Chain
Proxy only, HTTP mode only.
.B filters
is a global array of functions run in order on each request \fIbefore\fR a
backend is selected. Each filter either says nothing \(em carry on to the next
\(em or returns the answer, which ends the request there.
.PP
The ordering is the point. A request a filter refuses never reaches backend
selection, so no endpoint is resolved, no circuit breaker's half-open probe is
claimed, no pooled socket is spent and no connection is opened. This is where
authentication, rate limiting and header rewriting belong;
.B on_route
runs after a backend has already been chosen and answers a different question.
.PP
The chain is independent of
.BR \-\-strategy .
It runs whatever picks the backend afterwards, and on a proxy with no
.B on_route
at all. A proxy whose script declares no
.B filters
array pays nothing: the chain is guarded by the same callback mask as every
other hook.
.PP
Each filter is called with one argument:
.nf
method      string   HTTP method ("GET", "POST", ...)
path        string   Request path as the client sent it, this proxy's
                     prefix included \(em the same path on_route sees
version     string   HTTP version (e.g. "HTTP/1.1")
client_id   number   File descriptor of the client; pass it to
                     self.peer_ip() to identify the peer
headers     table    Request headers. Names are lowercased, values
                     are trimmed, and a field sent more than once is
                     joined with ", ".
.fi
.PP
And returns one of three things:
.TP
.B nil
(or nothing) \(em this filter has no opinion; the next one runs.
.TP
.BI "{ status = " N ", body = " string ", headers = " table " }"
The answer. The chain stops, the request is never forwarded, and this response
goes to the client.
.I status
must be 200\-599.
.I body
and
.I headers
are optional. The connection is closed afterwards, as it is for every other
response the proxy writes itself.
.TP
.BI "{ set_headers = " table " }"
Carry on, with these headers set on the request that goes to the backend. A
header set here replaces one the client sent under the same name rather than
joining it, and is emitted with a lowercased name.
.TP
.BI "{ remove_headers = { " name ", " name ", ... } }"
Carry on, with these headers taken off the request before it goes to the
backend \(em every occurrence of each name, so a field the client sent twice is
removed entirely. For a header the backend must never see whatever the client
sent: an internal trust header a caller forged, a cookie with no business past
the edge.
.I set_headers
and
.I remove_headers
may appear in the same table. A name that is both set and removed takes the
later of the two statements, exactly as two
.I set_headers
for one name resolve.
.PP
A filter that returns anything else, or that raises an error, refuses the
request with 500. A filter that did not say "carry on" is never treated as
though it had. An entry in
.B filters
that is not a function refuses the whole script at load time, for the same
reason: a silently dropped filter looks exactly like one that admits everybody.
.PP
Names a filter may not set \fIor remove\fR are dropped, with a line on stderr:
.B Content\-Length
and
.B Transfer\-Encoding
on either side, because the proxy forwards exactly the body the parsed request
declared and a filter that could change the length would make the proxy and the
backend disagree about where the request ends;
.B Host
on the request, because the proxy rewrites it to the endpoint it selected; and
the hop-by-hop set
.RB ( Connection ", " Keep\-Alive ", " TE ", " Trailer ", " Upgrade ", " Proxy\-* ).
So is any name that is not a bare token and any value carrying CR or LF \(em a
filter is frequently echoing something the client supplied.
.PP
Filters run on the event loop thread. Whatever a filter does, every request on
the proxy waits for: no blocking, no synchronous I/O, no long loops.
.PP
Example:
.nf
filters = {
    function(req)
        if not req.headers["authorization"] then
            return {
                status  = 401,
                body    = "authentication required\\n",
                headers = {["WWW-Authenticate"] = "Bearer"},
            }
        end
        return {set_headers = {["x-authenticated-user"] = "alice"}}
    end,
}
.fi
.IP
See
.B examples/proxy/filter\-chain.lua
for authentication, per-IP rate limiting and header rewriting as three filters
in one chain.
.SS Declared Routes
.B routes
is a global array in which a runtime states what it will answer: a path, and
the methods allowed on it. It is declared in the \fIhandler's\fR script and
published to the proxies that forward to that runtime, which then refuse those
methods at the edge \(em before any endpoint is resolved, any socket is taken
from the pool and any connection is opened.
.PP
The declaration lives with the handler on purpose. Configured on the proxy, a
method list is a second copy of something the handler already states in its own
code, in another runtime and soon in another process, and two copies drift: the
handler grows
.BR PATCH ,
the gateway goes on refusing it, and the 405 is explicable from neither side
alone. Nothing is configured on the proxy; editing the handler's script and
running
.B socketley reload\-lua
changes what the gateway enforces.
.PP
.nf
routes = {
    { path = "/endpoint/login", methods = {"POST", "GET"} },
    { path = "/static/*",       methods = {"GET"} },
}
.fi
.PP
.B path
is the path as the \fIhandler\fR sees it \(em the proxy's own
.BI / name /
prefix already stripped \(em and a query string is not part of it. A trailing
.B *
makes it a literal prefix; that is the whole of the pattern language. Exact
matches win over prefixes, and the longest prefix wins among prefixes.
.B methods
is a list of method names; a bare string is accepted for a single one. The
declaration is uppercased when the script loads, so
.B "methods = {\(dqpost\(dq}"
declares POST. The method a client sends is compared as sent \(em RFC 9110 9.1
makes the method token case-sensitive, and a request for
.B post
is one the handler would refuse too.
.PP
What the proxy does with it:
.TP
A declared method
is forwarded, as always.
.TP
An undeclared method on a declared path
is answered
.B 405 Method Not Allowed
with an
.B Allow
header listing what would have worked. No backend is involved.
.TP
.B HEAD
is allowed wherever
.B GET
is (RFC 9110 9.3.2) \(em a handler that serves GET serves HEAD whether or not it
listed it.
.TP
.B OPTIONS
is never refused. A plain OPTIONS \(em "what may I do here", RFC 9110 9.3.7 \(em
is answered
.B 204
with
.B Allow
out of the declaration. A CORS \fIpreflight\fR (an OPTIONS carrying
.B Origin
and
.BR Access\-Control\-Request\-Method )
is always forwarded to the handler instead: the declaration says nothing about
origins, and an answer without
.B Access\-Control\-Allow\-Origin
breaks the preflight exactly as a 405 would \(em the real request is never sent
and the browser reports a CORS error. A handler that declares
.B OPTIONS
itself always gets it.
.TP
A path nobody declared
is forwarded, whatever the method. A declaration speaks about the paths it names
and says nothing about the others; it is not a whitelist, and declaring one
route does not hide the rest.
.TP
A backend given as \fIhost:port\fR
has no declaration and never will \(em the published snapshot is keyed by
runtime name and by group. Such a backend is forwarded to as before.
.PP
For a proxy with several backends, or an
.BI @ group
backend, one rule covers both halves of the fold: nothing may be refused that
\fIeither\fR possible target might have served. A path only some of them declare
is dropped \(em a runtime that never mentioned it may well serve it. A path all
of them declare keeps the \fIunion\fR of their methods, since the proxy does not
know which target a request will reach. One backend or group member that
declares nothing therefore leaves that proxy enforcing nothing at all, silently
and on purpose: it has said nothing about what it answers, so nothing can be
refused on its account.
.PP
.B "Edge rejection is not input validation."
The proxy can confirm the method is one the handler declared. It cannot confirm
the body is a well-formed login, and it is not the only way in: a socketley
server listens on its own port, and a request that arrives there passed no edge
at all. A handler that stops checking its input because "the gateway checks" is
safe only for as long as every request comes through the gateway, which is a
property of the deployment and not of the code. Declare routes to save the
round trip and to say what you mean; go on validating.
.PP
.B socketley stats \fIproxy\fR
reports
.B declared_routes
\(em how many routes that proxy is actually enforcing. It is the first thing to
look at when a method that should be refused is not: a backend that declares
nothing, a backend named by address, a script that failed to load and a proxy
whose handler had not started yet all look identical from the client side, and
all of them read 0 here.
.PP
A malformed entry refuses the script at load time, with the reason on stderr,
rather than being dropped: a declaration that is not the one in the file is
invisible from both sides. A
.B routes
table with no entry at index 1 is refused too \(em it is an array, and
.B routes = {["/a"] = {"GET"}}
would otherwise load and declare nothing.
.PP
A proxy runtime running in a worker process of its own does not receive route
declarations yet and forwards every method; the declaration is enforced by
proxies running inside the daemon.
.IP
See
.B examples/proxy/declared\-routes.lua
for a handler that declares an AJAX endpoint and the gateway in front of it.
.SS Runtime Methods (in Lua)
.nf
self.name      -- Runtime name (read-only)
self.port      -- Port number (read-only)
self.state     -- Current state (read-only)
self.type      -- Runtime type (read-only)
self.protocol  -- "tcp" or "udp" (server/client only)
self.send(msg)             -- Send message (client)
self.send(client_id, msg)  -- Send to specific client (server)
self.broadcast(msg)        -- Broadcast to all clients (server)

-- Upstream actions (server with -u)
self.upstream_send(conn_id, msg) -- Send to a specific upstream
self.upstream_broadcast(msg)     -- Send to all connected upstreams
self.upstreams()                 -- Table of connected upstream conn_ids

-- Cache: strings
self.get(key)       -- Get string value
self.set(key, val)  -- Set string value
self.del(key)       -- Delete key (any type)

-- Cache: lists
self.lpush(key, val)        -- Push to front
self.rpush(key, val)        -- Push to back
self.lpop(key)              -- Pop from front
self.rpop(key)              -- Pop from back
self.llen(key)              -- List length

-- Cache: sets
self.sadd(key, member)      -- Add member
self.srem(key, member)      -- Remove member
self.sismember(key, member) -- Check membership
self.scard(key)             -- Set cardinality

-- Cache: hashes
self.hset(key, field, val)  -- Set hash field
self.hget(key, field)       -- Get hash field
self.hdel(key, field)       -- Delete hash field
self.hlen(key)              -- Hash field count

-- Cache: TTL
self.expire(key, seconds)   -- Set expiry
self.ttl(key)               -- Get remaining TTL
self.persist(key)           -- Remove expiry

-- Cache: pub/sub
self.publish(channel, msg)  -- Publish to channel

-- Server: connection control
self.disconnect(client_id)         -- Terminate a connection from Lua
self.peer_ip(client_id)            -- Get client IP (IPv4 or IPv6 string)
self.ws_headers(client_id)         -- Get WS upgrade headers table (nil for TCP)

-- Server: client enumeration and multicast
self.clients()                     -- Return table of all connected client_ids
self.multicast(ids, msg)           -- Send msg to a subset of clients (ids = table of client_ids)

-- Server: named groups
self.join_group(id, group)         -- Add client to a named group
self.leave_group(id, group)        -- Remove client from a group
self.group_send(group, msg)        -- Send msg to all clients in a group

-- Server: zero-copy pipe pairing (bypasses Lua callbacks)
self.pipe(id_a, id_b)             -- Pair two clients: data forwarded directly between them
self.unpipe(id)                    -- Remove pipe pairing for a client
socketley.pipe(src, src_fd, dst, dst_fd) -- Cross-runtime pipe (both must be servers)

-- Server: per-connection metadata
self.set_data(id, key, val)        -- Set string metadata on a connection (nil val deletes key)
self.get_data(id, key)             -- Get metadata string or nil

-- Server: client routing
self.route(client_id, target)      -- Forward client to sub-server
self.unroute(client_id)            -- Stop forwarding
self.get_route(client_id)          -- Get current route (string or nil)

-- Server: owner-targeted sending (sub-server only)
self.owner_send(client_id, msg)    -- Send to a connection routed here, through
                                   -- the runtime that owns it. Returns false if
                                   -- that connection has gone.
self.owner_broadcast(msg)          -- Broadcast to all owner's clients
.fi
.PP
.B self.owner_send()
takes only a client_id this runtime was actually given \(em one that arrived
through
.BR self.route() ,
reported by
.BR on_connect() .
It answers
.B false
for anything else, including a client_id whose connection has since closed. It
used to take any number and write it into the owner's connection table as a
descriptor index, so a script holding an id past its connection's life wrote
into whichever client had inherited that descriptor.
.SS Global Functions
.TP
.B socketley.log(msg)
Output log message with [lua] prefix.
.TP
.B socketley.set_timeout(ms, fn) \(-> timer_id
Fire
.I fn
once after
.I ms
milliseconds using an io_uring timeout SQE.
Returns an integer timer ID that can be passed to
.BR socketley.clear_timeout() .
Shares the event loop with the runtime \(em no extra threads.
.TP
.B socketley.set_interval(ms, fn) \(-> timer_id
Fire
.I fn
every
.I ms
milliseconds. Returns an integer timer ID that can be passed to
.BR socketley.clear_interval() .
.TP
.B socketley.clear_timeout(timer_id)
Cancel a pending timeout. The timer will not fire. Safe to call with
an already-expired or invalid ID (no-op).
.TP
.B socketley.clear_interval(timer_id)
Cancel a repeating interval. The timer stops after the current cycle
completes. Safe to call with an already-expired or invalid ID (no-op).
.TP
.B socketley.json_encode(value) \(-> string
Encode a Lua value (table, string, number, boolean, nil) as a JSON string.
Tables with consecutive integer keys starting at 1 are encoded as JSON
arrays; all others as objects.
.TP
.B socketley.json_decode(str) \(-> value
Decode a JSON string into a Lua value. Objects become tables with string
keys, arrays become tables with integer keys (1-based). Null becomes nil.
.TP
.B socketley.subscribe(cache_name, channel, fn)
Register
.I fn(channel, message)
to be called whenever the named cache runtime publishes to
.IR channel .
The function is called on the subscribing runtime's event loop turn (not from the cache itself).
Multiple subscriptions to the same channel are all fired in registration order.
.TP
.B socketley.http(opts) \(-> table
Synchronous HTTP/HTTPS client.
.B WARNING:
Blocks the event loop thread \(em the wait is bounded, but it is not
asynchronous: the whole exchange completes before any other runtime in
the daemon is served again. Use only in
.BR on_start / on_stop
or low-frequency
.B on_tick
callbacks.
.PP
.I opts
table keys:
.nf
url         string   Request URL (http:// or https://)
method      string   HTTP method (default "GET")
body        string   Request body (optional)
headers     table    Extra headers e.g. {["Content-Type"]="application/json"}
timeout_ms  number   Timeout in milliseconds (default 5000)
.fi
.PP
Returns a table:
.nf
ok      bool    true if HTTP status 2xx
status  number  HTTP status code (e.g. 200, 404)
body    string  Response body
error   string  Error message on failure
.fi
.PP
.I timeout_ms
is one deadline for the whole exchange \(em connect, TLS handshake,
request and response are all measured against it \(em not a separate
timeout per operation. Name resolution is the exception: it runs before
the deadline is armed and has no bound of its own. A response is refused
once it exceeds 8 MB rather than buffered without limit.
.PP
HTTPS uses OpenSSL and validates the peer. The certificate chain is
verified against the system CA store, and the certificate must have been
issued to the host in the URL \(em a host name is matched against the
certificate's DNS names with partial wildcards refused, an address
literal against its IP SAN. TLS 1.2 is the minimum accepted protocol
version. Failure of either check aborts the request with the reason in
.IR error ,
and there is no option to disable it, so a service presenting a
self-signed or private-CA certificate must have that CA in the trust
store OpenSSL reads (overridable with
.B SSL_CERT_FILE
or
.BR SSL_CERT_DIR ).
If no CA store is available at all the call fails immediately with
.BR "no system CA store available" .
.SS Runtime Management API
Create, start, stop, and remove runtimes from Lua scripts:
.nf
socketley.create(type, name, config)  -- Create runtime
socketley.start(name)                 -- Start runtime
socketley.stop(name)                  -- Stop runtime
socketley.remove(name)                -- Stop + remove runtime
socketley.send(name, msg)             -- Send/broadcast to runtime
socketley.list()                      -- List all runtime names (table)
socketley.get(name)                   -- Get info table or nil
.fi
.P
Config table keys: port, config (lua script path), target, mode, group, on_parent_stop ("remove"/"stop"), start (bool).
Lua-created runtimes have parent-child ownership; port=0 creates internal-only servers that receive forwarded messages only.
.SS Cluster Introspection API
When the daemon runs in cluster mode (\fB\-\-cluster\fR), the
.B socketley.cluster
table provides read-only introspection into the full cluster topology.
All functions return empty tables when not in cluster mode, so scripts work in both standalone and cluster setups without modification.
.TP
.B socketley.cluster.daemons() \(-> table
Returns an array of daemon info tables. Each entry has:
.nf
name      string   Daemon name (from \-\-name)
host      string   Advertised address (from \-\-cluster\-addr)
runtimes  number   Count of runtimes on this daemon
.fi
.IP
The local daemon is always the first entry. Remote daemons follow in discovery order.
.TP
.B socketley.cluster.runtimes() \(-> table
Returns a flat array of all runtimes across every daemon in the cluster:
.nf
daemon       string   Name of the daemon hosting this runtime
name         string   Runtime name
type         string   "server", "client", "proxy", or "cache"
port         number   Listen port (0 = internal-only)
group        string   Group tag (empty string if unset)
state        string   "created", "running", "stopped", or "failed"
connections  number   Current connection count
.fi
.TP
.B socketley.cluster.group(name) \(-> table
Returns running members of the named group that have a valid port.
Each entry has:
.nf
daemon       string   Daemon name
host         string   Advertised address
port         number   Listen port
connections  number   Current connection count
.fi
.IP
Useful for building custom routing, health checks, or failover logic from Lua.
.TP
.B socketley.cluster.stats() \(-> table
Aggregate cluster statistics:
.nf
daemons   number   Total daemon count (local + remote)
runtimes  number   Total runtime count across all daemons
running   number   Count of runtimes in "running" state
groups    table    Maps group name (string) \(-> member count (number)
.fi
.SS Cluster Event Callbacks
These optional callbacks fire when the cluster topology changes.
Events are detected during the 2-second scan/publish cycle, so callbacks
may fire up to 2 seconds after the actual change.
A daemon is considered stale and triggers
.B on_cluster_leave
after 10 seconds without a heartbeat update.
.TP
.B on_cluster_join(daemon)
Fired when a new remote daemon is discovered in the cluster directory.
.I daemon
is a table with
.B name
(string) and
.B host
(string) fields.
.TP
.B on_cluster_leave(daemon)
Fired when a remote daemon's heartbeat file becomes stale (>10s old) or is
removed from the cluster directory.
.I daemon
is a table with a
.B name
(string) field.
.TP
.B on_group_change(group, members)
Fired when the total member count of a group changes (a runtime joins or
leaves the group, or a daemon with group members appears/disappears).
Only fires on actual count changes, not on every scan cycle.
.I group
is the group name (string).
.I members
is an array of tables, each with
.BR daemon ", " host ", and " port
fields representing the current group membership.
.PP
Example \(em log topology changes and maintain a local routing table:
.nf
local backends = {}

function on_cluster_join(daemon)
    socketley.log("daemon joined: " .. daemon.name .. " at " .. daemon.host)
end

function on_cluster_leave(daemon)
    socketley.log("daemon left: " .. daemon.name)
end

function on_group_change(group, members)
    if group == "api" then
        backends = members   \-\- update routing table
        socketley.log("api group now has " .. #members .. " members")
    end
end

function on_start()
    \-\- Initial snapshot
    local stats = socketley.cluster.stats()
    socketley.log("cluster: " .. stats.daemons .. " daemons, "
                  .. stats.running .. " running runtimes")
end
.fi
.SH DASHBOARD
When
.B config.metrics_port
is set in the daemon configuration file, the metrics endpoint serves
multiple routes:
.TP
.B GET /
Live web dashboard. Auto-refreshes every 2 seconds. Shows all runtimes,
connection counts, throughput, and uptime in a browser-friendly view.
.TP
.B GET /metrics
Prometheus exposition format (existing). Suitable for scraping by
Prometheus, Grafana Agent, or similar monitoring stacks.
.TP
.B GET /api/overview
JSON object with high-level daemon statistics:
.nf
{
    "runtimes": 5,
    "connections": 142,
    "uptime_seconds": 86400,
    "throughput_in": 12345678,
    "throughput_out": 9876543
}
.fi
.TP
.B GET /api/runtimes
JSON array of all runtimes with name, type, port, state, connections,
and message/byte counters.
.TP
.BI "GET /api/runtime/" name
JSON object for a single runtime identified by
.IR name .
Returns 404 if the runtime does not exist.
.PP
The
.B dashboard
CLI command prints the dashboard URL:
.nf
$ socketley dashboard
Dashboard: http://localhost:9100/
.fi
.SH SERVICE MESH
The proxy runtime includes service-mesh primitives that can be composed
to build sidecar proxies, API gateways, or resilient service-to-service
communication layers.
.SS Health Checks
Active health checking probes backends at a regular interval
.RB ( \-\-health\-interval )
using either a TCP connect or an HTTP GET
.RB ( \-\-health\-check ).
A backend is marked unhealthy after
.B \-\-health\-threshold
consecutive failures and is excluded from the load balancer pool.
A single successful check restores the backend.
.SS Circuit Breaker
The circuit breaker prevents cascading failures by tracking consecutive
errors per backend. It has three states:
.TP
.B closed
Normal operation. Requests are forwarded to the backend. Errors are
counted.
.TP
.B open
Triggered when errors reach
.BR \-\-circuit\-threshold .
All requests fail immediately without attempting a connection. After
.B \-\-circuit\-timeout
seconds, the circuit moves to half-open.
.TP
.B half-open
A single probe request is allowed through \(em one, not one per
selection: while that probe is outstanding the backend is unavailable to
everyone else. If it succeeds, the circuit returns to
.BR closed .
If it fails, the circuit re-opens.
.SS Response Deadline
A backend that accepts connections and then stops answering fails no
check: a TCP health probe connects to it successfully, and a request
that never completes is counted as neither an error nor a success, so
the circuit breaker has nothing to open on.
.B \-\-backend\-timeout
(default 2 seconds) bounds the wait for the first byte of a response and
records the expiry as a backend error, which is what puts such a backend
through the breaker like any other failure. The request is retried
elsewhere if
.B \-\-retry
allows it and answered
.B 504 Gateway Timeout
otherwise.
.PP
The deadline covers the handshake as well as the answer: a backend that
never completes its connect inside it is answered
.B 504 Gateway Timeout
too, rather than the client being closed without a response. A request
routed through an
.B @group
backend is charged to the member it actually reached, so a group member
that stalls is taken out of rotation by the same breaker as a listed
backend.
.PP
The deadline is on by default and only covers the wait for the first
byte, so a large or streaming response body is unaffected. An endpoint
that legitimately needs more than 2 seconds to start answering has to be
given a longer
.B \-\-backend\-timeout
or
.BR "\-\-backend\-timeout 0" ;
otherwise the proxy treats it as hung. It applies to HTTP mode only \(em
in TCP mode there is no request boundary to measure from.
.SS Request Framing
In HTTP mode the proxy is strict about how a request's header block is
framed, and refuses anything it and a backend could read differently.
The proxy forwards bytes to a backend that parses them independently; if
the two disagree about where a request ends, the next request on the
same connection starts in the wrong place, which is request smuggling.
Normalising the request instead would mean guessing which way the
backend will read it, so the proxy declines to guess.
.PP
A request is answered
.B 400 Bad Request
and its connection is closed, without contacting any backend, when the
header block contains any of:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
a bare LF or a bare CR \(em every line must end with CRLF
.IP \(bu 2
whitespace between a field name and its colon
.RB ( "Content\-Length : 6" )
.IP \(bu 2
a continuation (obs\-fold) line, i.e. one starting with a space or tab
.IP \(bu 2
a field line with no colon
.IP \(bu 2
both
.B Content\-Length
and
.BR Transfer\-Encoding ,
or more than one
.B Content\-Length
field
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B Content\-Length
that is not a whole decimal number
.RB ( "Content\-Length: 6x" )
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B Transfer\-Encoding
whose final coding is not
.B chunked
.RB ( "Transfer\-Encoding: gzip" ,
.BR "Transfer\-Encoding: chunked, gzip" ,
or
.B chunked
listed twice) \(em such a request has no determinable body length at all
.RE
.PP
Every request on a connection is framed and checked on its own, including
one pipelined behind another. Only the body a request declares travels
with it: bytes past its
.B Content\-Length
are not body, and are held back and parsed as the next request rather
than forwarded. Pipelining still works \(em a second request sent before
the first is answered is served \(em but it is answered in turn, after
the same checks, and a smuggling attempt hidden behind a clean request is
refused exactly as it would be if it had been sent first. A request held
this way is subject to the same 10 second deadline as a half\-arrived one:
if the exchange in front of it never reaches an end this proxy can point
at \(em a backend whose response is framed in a way it cannot read \(em the
held request is answered
.B 408 Request Timeout
rather than left waiting. A
.B chunked
request is the one body this proxy cannot measure, because it never
de\-chunks; its body is forwarded as an opaque stream, so a request
pipelined behind a chunked one is framed by the backend alone.
.PP
Compliant clients emit none of these. A request whose header block is
still arriving is held until the terminating blank line, subject to the
16 KiB header limit
.RB ( "431 Request Header Fields Too Large" )
and a 10 second header deadline
.RB ( "408 Request Timeout" ).
.PP
The header deadline starts at the first byte of a request and ends when
its header block does. It is fixed, is not configurable, and is
deliberately independent of
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout ,
which defaults to 0 (never) and so cannot be relied on to end a
half\-sent request: a client that sends a request line and then stops
sends no further bytes for the 16 KiB limit to count, and would
otherwise hold its connection, its descriptor and its 64 KB buffer for
as long as it liked. Neither an idle keep\-alive connection between
requests nor a slow request body is measured against it \(em only the
gap between a request's first byte and its terminating blank line.
.PP
The same strictness is applied to backend responses, but only to decide
whether the backend connection may be reused: a response whose length
cannot be established with certainty \(em because its header block was
split across reads, or was not strictly CRLF-framed, or because more
bytes arrived than the length it declared \(em is forwarded to
the client unchanged, and its connection is closed instead of returned
to the pool.
.PP
A connection already in the pool is checked before it is handed to the
next request, and one with unread data on it is closed rather than
reused. Nothing was asked of an idle pooled socket, so anything waiting
on it is something the backend sent unbidden \(em the tail of an answer
whose length was mis\-declared, or a second answer volunteered behind the
first. Reusing it would make those bytes the head of the next client's
response, so the connection is dropped and a fresh one opened. The proxy
logs this once per instance.
.PP
Reuse covers an
.B @group
backend as well as a listed one. A pooled connection is filed under the
endpoint it was opened to, never under the group, so it is only ever
handed back to a request routed to that same member; a member that leaves
the group has its pooled connections closed at once rather than left to
the idle sweep.
.PP
It does not cover a
.B TLS
backend. The pool is keyed by host and port, and a TLS connection carries more
than an address \(em a negotiated session, a peer identity that was verified
against a name, and possibly a client certificate \(em none of which that key
can express. Handing one to the next request for the same endpoint would be a
guess about identity, so such connections are closed after their response and
each request to a TLS backend pays for a fresh handshake.
.SS Retries
When
.B \-\-retry
is set, failed requests are automatically retried on the next backend
in the load balancer pool. By default only idempotent methods are
retried; use
.B \-\-retry\-all
to include POST and PATCH.
.SS mTLS
Both legs can be encrypted.
.BR "\-\-tls \-\-cert \-\-key"
terminates TLS on the proxy's own port and
.B \-\-client\-ca
makes it demand a certificate from its callers;
.B https://
on a backend address, or any of
.BR \-\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.BR \-\-client\-key ,
takes the backend leg to TLS, with the chain and the endpoint's name verified
and a client certificate presented when one is configured. A full sidecar pair
looks like this:
.nf
socketley create proxy edge -p 8443 \\
    \-\-tls \-\-cert edge.pem \-\-key edge\-key.pem \\
    \-\-client\-ca mesh\-ca.pem \\
    \-\-backend https://10.0.0.7:8443 \\
    \-\-ca mesh\-ca.pem \\
    \-\-client\-cert edge.pem \-\-client\-key edge\-key.pem \\
    \-\-sidecar \-s
.fi
.PP
Two things to plan for: a TLS backend connection is never returned to the
connection pool, so every request to one pays for a handshake, and
.B \-\-health\-check http
is refused against a TLS backend \(em use
.BR "\-\-health\-check tcp" .
See
.B "mTLS Flags"
under
.B "PROXY FLAGS"
for what is verified and what cannot be switched off.
.SS Sidecar Deployment
The
.B \-\-sidecar
flag enables a production-ready default set: TCP health checks, circuit
breaker (threshold 5, timeout 30s), 2 retries, and graceful drain. Deploy
alongside your application:
.nf
socketley create proxy myapp \-\-sidecar \\
    \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 -p 8080 -s
.fi
.PP
The proxy listens on port 8080 and forwards to the local application on
port 3000. Unhealthy backends are automatically removed, and the circuit
breaker prevents thundering-herd retries during outages.
.PP
.B \-\-sidecar
does not change
.BR \-\-backend\-timeout ,
which is 2 seconds by default and charges its expiry to the circuit
breaker that
.B \-\-sidecar
enables. If the application can take longer than that to send its first
response byte, say so explicitly:
.nf
socketley create proxy myapp \-\-sidecar \-\-backend\-timeout 30 \\
    \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 -p 8080 -s
.fi
.SH RUNTIME STATES
.TP
.B created
Initial state after creation.
.TP
.B running
Active and processing I/O.
.TP
.B stopped
Stopped but can be restarted.
.TP
.B failed
Setup failed, cannot recover.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a simple server (daemon is managed by systemd when installed):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server myserver -p 9000 -s
.fi
.PP
Create client connecting to server:
.PP
.nf
socketley create client myclient -t 127.0.0.1:9000 -s
.fi
.PP
Create HTTP proxy with load balancing:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy gateway -p 8080 \\
    --backend 127.0.0.1:9001,127.0.0.1:9002 \\
    --strategy round-robin -s
.fi
.PP
Server with bash output (messages to stdout):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server echo -p 9000 -b -s
# With timestamp and prefix:
socketley create server echo -p 9000 -bpt -s
.fi
.PP
Send message to runtime:
.PP
.nf
socketley send myserver "Hello from CLI"
socketley send myclient "Message to server"
.fi
.PP
Create persistent cache with maxmemory:
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache store -p 9000 \\
    --persistent /data/cache.bin \\
    --maxmemory 256M --eviction allkeys-lru -s
.fi
.PP
Create cache with RESP mode (Redis-compatible):
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache redis-compat -p 6379 --resp -s
redis-cli -p 6379 SET mykey myval
redis-cli -p 6379 GET mykey
.fi
.PP
Server with connection limits and rate limiting:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server api -p 9000 \\
    --max-connections 100 --rate-limit 1000 -s
.fi
.PP
View runtime statistics:
.PP
.nf
socketley stats myserver
# Output: name, type, port, connections, total_messages, bytes_in/out, ...
.fi
.PP
Restart a running runtime:
.PP
.nf
socketley reload myserver
# Stops and restarts the runtime
socketley reload *
# Restarts all running runtimes
.fi
.PP
Hot-reload Lua script:
.PP
.nf
socketley reload-lua myserver
# Re-loads the Lua script without stopping the runtime
socketley reload-lua *
# Hot-reloads Lua on all runtimes with scripts
.fi
.PP
Start/stop all runtimes:
.PP
.nf
socketley start *
# Starts all non-running runtimes
socketley stop *
# Stops all running runtimes
socketley remove *
# Stops all and removes everything
.fi
.PP
Multiple names and glob patterns:
.PP
.nf
socketley stop myserver myclient
# Stops both runtimes
socketley stop my*
# Stops all runtimes starting with "my"
socketley stats cache?
# Shows stats for cache1, cache2, etc.
socketley start myserver cache*
# Starts myserver and all cache-prefixed runtimes
socketley remove api-* test-*
# Removes all api- and test- prefixed runtimes
.fi
.PP
Interactive mode (live terminal session):
.PP
.nf
# Start server and enter interactive session
socketley create server echo -p 9000
socketley start echo -i
# Type messages to broadcast, see received messages
# Press Ctrl+C to detach (server keeps running)

# Attach to already-running cache
socketley start mycache -i
# Type cache commands: set foo bar, get foo, etc.
.fi
.PP
Interactive edit (opens JSON config in $EDITOR):
.PP
.nf
socketley edit myserver           # Opens config in editor
socketley edit myserver -r        # Opens editor, auto-reloads Lua after save
socketley edit myserver -p 9001   # Flag-based edit (direct)
.fi
.PP
Cache replication (leader-follower):
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache leader -p 9000 -s
socketley create cache follower -p 9001 --replicate 127.0.0.1:9000 -s
.fi
.PP
Encrypted server. The listener terminates TLS itself; everything above it \(em
line protocol, HTTP, WebSocket, RESP \(em runs on the decrypted stream, so
this same runtime serves
.B https://
and
.B wss://
on the one port:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server secure -p 9443 \\
    --tls --cert server.pem --key server-key.pem -s
curl --cacert server.pem https://localhost:9443/
.fi
.PP
Mutual TLS \(em a client must present a certificate signed by the given CA:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server mtls -p 9443 \\
    --tls --cert server.pem --key server-key.pem \\
    --ca trusted-clients.pem -s
.fi
.PP
Renewing a certificate: the files are read at start, so
.B reload
picks up a new one.
.PP
.nf
socketley reload secure
.fi
.PP
An incomplete configuration is refused rather than served in the clear:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server oops -p 9443 --tls --cert server.pem
# --tls: a TLS listener needs --key <file> to go with --cert
# exit status 1
.fi
.PP
Encrypted client. The dial is the mirror of the listener above: one flag turns
the transport on, and the peer is verified on both counts \(em the chain
against the trust store, and the certificate against the host in
.BR \-t .
.PP
.nf
# verify against the system trust store
socketley create client feed -t events.example.com:9443 --tls -s

# verify against a private CA instead
socketley create client feed -t 10.0.0.7:9443 \\
    --tls --ca internal-ca.pem -s

# and present a certificate of our own, for a peer that demands one
socketley create client feed -t 10.0.0.7:9443 \\
    --tls --ca internal-ca.pem \\
    --cert client.pem --key client-key.pem -s
.fi
.PP
A peer whose certificate does not verify costs the socket \(em the client
re-dials on its backoff and never falls back to cleartext. There is no
skip-verify flag: encryption without verification is protection against
somebody recording the wire and none at all against somebody sitting on it,
and the two look identical from the outside.
.PP
The same rules apply as on the listener. Half a certificate pair is refused,
a trust store without
.B \-\-tls
is refused, and
.B reload
is how a renewed client certificate is installed:
.PP
.nf
socketley create client bad -t host:9443 --tls --cert client.pem
# --cert: needs --key <file> as well -- a certificate with no key
# presents nothing
# exit status 1
.fi
.PP
Graceful shutdown (drain pending writes):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server api -p 9000 --drain -s
socketley stop api   # Flushes write queues before closing
.fi
.PP
UDP server (fire-and-forget messaging):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server metrics -p 9000 --udp -b -s
echo "cpu.load 0.42" | socat - UDP:localhost:9000
.fi
.PP
UDP client:
.PP
.nf
socketley create client udpcli -t 127.0.0.1:9000 --udp -s
.fi
.PP
Create read-only cache (for shared lookups):
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache lookup -p 9000 --mode readonly -s
.fi
.PP
Master mode server (one controller, many viewers):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server broadcast -p 9000 \\
    --mode master --master-pw secret -s
# Client A: sends "master secret" → "master: ok"
# Client A: sends "hello" → broadcast to all others
# Client B: sends "hello" → silently dropped
.fi
.PP
Master mode with forwarding:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server ctrl -p 9000 \\
    --mode master --master-pw admin123 --master-forward -s
# Non-master messages forwarded to master as "[<fd>] msg"
.fi
.PP
WebSocket server (auto-detected, no flags needed):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server ws -p 9000 -s
# Browser: new WebSocket("ws://localhost:9000")
# Raw TCP clients also work on the same port
.fi
.PP
Cache access through server:
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache db -p 9001 -s
socketley create server api -p 9000 --cache db -s
# Client sends: "cache set user:1 alice" → "ok"
# Client sends: "cache get user:1" → "alice"
# Client sends: "hello" → broadcast as usual
.fi
.PP
Dynamic proxy with group-based backend discovery:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server api1 -p 9001 -g api -s
socketley create server api2 -p 9002 -g api -s
socketley create proxy gw -p 8080 --backend @api -s
# Proxy routes to all running members of group "api"

# Add a new member dynamically (no proxy restart needed)
socketley create server api3 -p 9003 -g api -s

# Mix group and direct backends
socketley create proxy gw2 -p 8081 \\
    --backend @api,10.0.0.5:9000 -s
.fi
.PP
Lua HTTP worker (auto-detected, no flags needed):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server api -p 9000 --lua handler.lua -s
# handler.lua:
# function on_http_request(req)
#     return {status=200, headers={["Content-Type"]="application/json"},
#             body='{"hello":"world"}'}
# end
# curl http://localhost:9000/api
.fi
.PP
Sidecar proxy with health checks and circuit breaker:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy sidecar -p 8080 \-\-sidecar \\
    \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 -s
.fi
.PP
Proxy with HTTP health checks and retries:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy gateway -p 8080 \\
    \-\-backend 10.0.0.1:9000,10.0.0.2:9000 \\
    \-\-health\-check http \-\-health\-path /ready \\
    \-\-health\-interval 10 \-\-health\-threshold 5 \\
    \-\-retry 3 \-\-circuit\-threshold 10 -s
.fi
.PP
Proxy with mTLS on both legs. The listener terminates TLS and demands a
certificate from its callers; the backend leg dials TLS, verifies the chain
and the endpoint's name, and presents a certificate of its own:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy secure \-p 8443 \\
    \-\-tls \-\-cert proxy.pem \-\-key proxy\-key.pem \\
    \-\-client\-ca trusted\-clients.pem \\
    \-\-client\-cert backend\-client.pem \-\-client\-key backend\-client\-key.pem \\
    \-\-ca mesh\-ca.pem \\
    \-\-backend https://10.0.0.1:9000 -s
# Backend connections are not pooled: one handshake per request.
# --health-check http is refused against a TLS backend; use tcp.
.fi
.PP
TLS to the backend only \(em the proxy listens in cleartext on loopback and
encrypts the hop that leaves the machine:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy egress \-p 8080 \\
    \-\-backend https://api.example.com \-s
# Verified against the system CA store, name checked against the certificate.
# A private CA goes in --ca; there is no way to skip verification.
.fi
.PP
Open the live dashboard:
.PP
.nf
socketley dashboard
# Dashboard: http://localhost:9100/
# Open in browser for live runtime view
.fi
.PP
Query dashboard API:
.PP
.nf
curl http://localhost:9100/api/overview
curl http://localhost:9100/api/runtimes
curl http://localhost:9100/api/runtime/myserver
.fi
.PP
Load complex configuration:
.PP
.nf
socketley --lua /etc/socketley/setup.lua
.fi
.SH CLUSTER MODE
Multi-daemon cluster discovery allows proxies to route to runtimes on remote
daemons. Each daemon publishes its state to a shared directory (typically a
Docker volume) every 2 seconds. Stale entries (heartbeat older than 10s) are
automatically excluded.
.PP
.B How it works:
.RS
.IP 1. 3
Each daemon writes its state to
.IR dir / name .json
.IP 2. 3
Every 2 seconds, each daemon scans the directory for peer files
.IP 3. 3
Proxy
.B @group
resolution includes both local and remote group members
.IP 4. 3
On graceful shutdown, the daemon deletes its file
.RE
.PP
.B Docker Compose example:
.PP
.nf
version: "3.8"
volumes:
  cluster:

services:
  api1:
    image: socketley
    command: >
      sh -c "socketley daemon &
             sleep 1 &&
             socketley daemon --name api1 --cluster /cluster &&
             socketley create server web -p 9001 -g api -s && wait"
    volumes: [cluster:/cluster]

  api2:
    image: socketley
    command: >
      sh -c "socketley daemon &
             sleep 1 &&
             socketley daemon --name api2 --cluster /cluster &&
             socketley create server web -p 9001 -g api -s && wait"
    volumes: [cluster:/cluster]

  gateway:
    image: socketley
    command: >
      sh -c "socketley daemon &
             sleep 1 &&
             socketley daemon --name gateway --cluster /cluster &&
             sleep 1 &&
             socketley create proxy gw -p 8080 --backend @api -s && wait"
    volumes: [cluster:/cluster]
    ports: ["8080:8080"]
.fi
.PP
Alternatively, use a Lua config file:
.PP
.nf
\-\- /etc/socketley/setup.lua
daemon = { name = "api1", cluster = "/cluster" }
runtimes = {
    { type = "server", name = "web", port = 9001, group = "api", start = true }
}
.fi
.PP
.nf
socketley daemon &
sleep 1 && socketley --lua /etc/socketley/setup.lua
.fi
.PP
.B Inspecting the cluster:
.PP
.nf
socketley cluster ls
socketley cluster ps
socketley cluster group api
socketley cluster stats
socketley cluster watch
.fi
.PP
.B Stale detection:
.TS
l l l.
Scenario	Behavior	Recovery
_
Graceful shutdown	File deleted	Immediate
Container killed	Stale heartbeat	~10 seconds
Container restart	File overwritten	Immediate
.TE
.SH INSTALLATION
.SS Quick Install
.nf
sudo bash packaging/install.sh
sudo systemctl start socketley
.fi
.SS Debian Package
.nf
bash packaging/build-deb.sh 1.0.9
sudo dpkg -i socketley_1.0.9_amd64.deb
sudo systemctl start socketley
.fi
.SS Uninstall
.nf
sudo bash packaging/uninstall.sh
.fi
.SH STATE PERSISTENCE
Runtime configurations are persisted as JSON files. When a runtime is created,
its configuration is saved. When the daemon starts, all persisted runtimes are
restored. Runtimes that were running when the daemon stopped are auto-started.
.PP
This means runtimes survive daemon restarts and system reboots (like Docker
with restart-always policy).
.PP
State is saved after create, start, stop, remove, and edit commands.
The
.B was_running
field tracks whether a runtime should be auto-started on daemon startup.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Read at daemon startup; all are optional. The first selects which daemon this
is. The rest are tuning knobs whose defaults suit a general-purpose deployment
and only need changing for an unusual workload.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
Which daemon you are, or which one you are talking to. Overrides the default
lookup
.RB ( /run/socketley/socketley.sock
when installed and privileged, otherwise
.BR /tmp/socketley.sock ).
.IP
No workload calls for setting it. A deployed node — a container or a host — has
a filesystem namespace to itself, so one daemon on the default path is the whole
arrangement, and that is what the systemd unit ships. Clustering does not use
this variable either: members publish themselves into a shared directory and
reach each other over network addresses, never over socket paths. See
.BR "CLUSTER MODE" .
.IP
What it is for is running several daemons inside
.I one
namespace, which is the development and test situation and nothing else. Two
checkouts on one workstation, or two test runs at once, are otherwise the same
daemon: whichever process binds the default path first owns it, and every later
one silently talks to that process instead of starting its own. Anything that
starts a daemon on the default path is claiming the identity the real daemon
runs under, so the test suite gives every test a socket of its own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_SQPOLL=1
Give every io_uring ring in this daemon \(em its own, and every worker process it
forks \(em a submission-queue polling kernel thread. Unset by default, which means
no such thread exists.
.IP
SQPOLL makes a submission a store to a queue tail with no system call at all,
which is worth having on a runtime that is saturating a link. It costs one
kernel thread per ring, held for as long as the ring exists and spinning on the
queue whether or not anything is being submitted, which is worth nothing at all
on a runtime that is waiting for its next connection. It was previously always
on; on a machine running several mostly-idle runtimes that was a polling thread
each, doing nothing.
.IP
A ring's polling mode is fixed by the kernel when the ring is created, so this
is read once at daemon startup and cannot be changed afterwards. The per-runtime
.B \-\-sqpoll
flag states what a runtime requires and is refused at
.B create
time when this daemon's ring does not provide it.
.IP
.B "socketley daemon \-\-sqpoll"
and
.B \-\-no\-sqpoll
say the same thing on the command line and win when both are given. The variable
remains the only route into a worker process, which has no command line of its
own and inherits the daemon's environment.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CPUS=\fImax\fR|\fI0\-3\fR|\fI0,1,2,3\fR
The set of CPUs the daemon's I/O thread may run on, and \(em when SQPOLL is on \(em
the set whose lowest member carries the polling kernel thread. Default
.BR max ,
which applies no pinning at all rather than a mask of the CPUs that happen to be
online now.
.IP
A CPU this machine does not have, or a set that comes out empty, is refused and
the daemon runs unpinned with a warning; it is never rounded up to
.BR max .
.IP
.BI "socketley daemon \-\-cpus " set
says the same thing on the command line and wins when both are given. There the
same impossible set is refused outright and the daemon does not start, because a
flag typed for this one run is a requirement rather than an inherited default.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_SQPOLL_IDLE_MS
How long the io_uring SQPOLL kernel thread keeps polling the submission queue
before it sleeps, in milliseconds. Default
.BR 10 .
Has no effect unless
.B SOCKETLEY_SQPOLL
is set.
Any periodic timer that fires faster than this window prevents the thread from
ever sleeping, which costs a full CPU core even at zero traffic. Raising it only
helps workloads that sit idle for long stretches and then burst; under sustained
load the queue never drains and the thread stays awake regardless.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CACHE_TTL_SWEEP_MS
Interval between periodic sweeps for expired cache keys, in milliseconds.
Default
.BR 500 .
This never affects correctness: an expired key is not served no matter when the
sweep runs, because every read path checks the expiry itself. It controls only
how quickly keys that are never accessed again release their memory. Keep it
comfortably above
.B SOCKETLEY_SQPOLL_IDLE_MS
so the poller can sleep between sweeps.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CACHE_BUF_SIZE
Size in bytes of each buffer in a cache runtime's io_uring provided-buffer ring.
Default
.BR 4096 .
Larger buffers let one read completion carry more commands, but a read that
returns less than a full buffer still consumes the whole one, so the waste per
partial read grows with the size. Measured on the dev VM, raising this to 16384
cost 31% at 1 KB values and 8% at 4 KB values — the default is where it belongs
unless a specific workload shows otherwise.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CACHE_BUF_COUNT
Number of buffers in that ring. Default
.BR 512 .
Together with the size this bounds how much concurrent traffic the runtime can
absorb before the pool runs dry and reads fall back to a slower per-read path;
the pool costs count times size of memory per cache runtime.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_NAPI_BUSY_POLL_US
Enable io_uring NAPI busy polling with this timeout in microseconds. Unset by
default. Trades CPU for lower receive latency on busy links.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_SQPOLL_CPU=\fIN\fR
Pin the SQPOLL kernel thread to CPU
.IR N ,
overriding the CPU
.B SOCKETLEY_CPUS
would have chosen for it. Unset by default. Needed only for the one arrangement
.B SOCKETLEY_CPUS
cannot express \(em a poller deliberately outside the set its loop thread runs on.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CONFIG
Path to the daemon configuration file.
.SH FILES
.SS System Mode (installed via package)
.TP
.I /usr/bin/socketley
Binary.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/socketley.service
systemd unit file.
.TP
.I /run/socketley/socketley.sock
Unix domain socket for daemon IPC.
.TP
.I /var/lib/socketley/runtimes/
Per-runtime JSON configuration files.
.TP
.I /etc/socketley/config.lua
Daemon configuration (log_level, metrics_port).
.SS Development Mode (no system install)
.TP
.I /tmp/socketley.sock
Unix domain socket for daemon IPC.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/socketley/runtimes/
Per-runtime JSON configuration files.
.TP
.I ~/.config/socketley/config.lua
Daemon configuration (or set SOCKETLEY_CONFIG env var).
.SS Daemon Configuration
The daemon config file is a Lua script that sets a
.B config
table:
.nf
config = {
    log_level = "info",      -- debug, info, warn, error
    metrics_port = 9100      -- Prometheus metrics HTTP endpoint
}
.fi
.PP
When
.B metrics_port
is set, the daemon starts an HTTP endpoint that serves a live web dashboard
at
.BR "GET /" ,
Prometheus exposition format metrics at
.BR "GET /metrics" ,
and JSON API endpoints at
.BR "/api/overview" ", " "/api/runtimes" ", and " "/api/runtime/<name>"
(see
.BR DASHBOARD ).
Metrics include: runtimes total/running, connections total/active,
messages total, bytes in/out, and per-runtime breakdowns.
.SH C++ SDK
Socketley ships a three-tier modular C++ SDK in
.BR include/linux/socketley/ .
Tiers 1 and 3 are fully self-contained header-only files \(em copy them anywhere,
compile with
.BR "g++ -std=c++17" ,
no library needed. Tier 2 requires building from source.
.PP
.TS
l l l l .
Tier	Header	Use Case	Dependencies
_
1	socketley/control.h	Control a running daemon	None (POSIX)
2	socketley/{server,cache,proxy,client}.h	Embed the full engine	libsocketley_sdk.a
3	socketley/attach.h	Register with a daemon	None (Tier 1)
.TE
.SS Tier 1: Daemon Control (header-only)
.B socketley/control.h
provides full programmatic control over a running daemon through the same Unix
socket IPC that the CLI uses. Zero dependencies beyond POSIX.
.PP
.B "Result type:"
.nf
struct socketley::result {
    int         exit_code;  // 0=success, 1=bad input, 2=fatal, -1=connect fail
    std::string data;       // response body
};
.fi
.PP
.B "Runtime management functions"
(all in namespace
.BR socketley::ctl ):
.nf
command(cmd)               // raw command (glob patterns work)
command(socket_path, cmd)  // custom socket path
create(type, name, flags)  // "server", "client", "proxy", "cache"
start(name)                stop(name)
remove(name)               send(name, message)
ls()   ps()   stats(name)  show(name)
reload(name)               reload_lua(name)
edit(name, flags)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache string operations"
(direct IPC, no TCP connection needed):
.nf
cache_get(cache, key)           cache_set(cache, key, value)
cache_del(cache, key)           cache_exists(cache, key)
cache_incr(cache, key)          cache_decr(cache, key)
cache_incrby(cache, key, n)     cache_decrby(cache, key, n)
cache_append(cache, key, val)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache list operations:"
.nf
cache_lpush(cache, key, val)    cache_rpush(cache, key, val)
cache_lpop(cache, key)          cache_rpop(cache, key)
cache_llen(cache, key)          cache_lrange(cache, key, start, stop)
cache_lindex(cache, key, idx)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache set operations:"
.nf
cache_sadd(cache, key, member)  cache_srem(cache, key, member)
cache_sismember(cache, key, m)  cache_scard(cache, key)
cache_smembers(cache, key)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache hash operations:"
.nf
cache_hset(cache, key, f, v)    cache_hget(cache, key, field)
cache_hdel(cache, key, field)   cache_hlen(cache, key)
cache_hgetall(cache, key)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache TTL:"
.nf
cache_expire(cache, key, sec)   cache_ttl(cache, key)
cache_persist(cache, key)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache pub/sub:"
.nf
cache_publish(cache, ch, msg)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache admin:"
.nf
cache_size(cache)               cache_memory(cache)
cache_keys(cache, pattern)
cache_flush(cache [, path])     cache_load(cache [, path])
.fi
.PP
Socket path resolution: checks
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
env, then
.BR /run/socketley/socketley.sock ,
then
.BR /tmp/socketley.sock .
Set the variable only to reach a daemon that is not the one for this namespace,
which in practice means a second daemon on a development machine; a deployed
node runs one daemon on the default path.
.PP
.B "Example: lifecycle management"
.nf
#include <socketley/control.h>
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    auto r = socketley::ctl::create("server", "myapp", "-p 9000 -s");
    if (r.exit_code != 0) return 1;

    socketley::ctl::send("myapp", "Hello from C++!");
    auto stats = socketley::ctl::stats("myapp");
    printf("%s\\n", stats.data.c_str());

    socketley::ctl::stop("myapp");
    socketley::ctl::remove("myapp");
}
// g++ -std=c++17 myapp.cpp -Iinclude/linux -o myapp
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: fleet management with globs"
.nf
for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
    std::string name  = "api_" + std::to_string(i);
    std::string flags = "-p " + std::to_string(9000+i) + " -s";
    socketley::ctl::create("server", name, flags);
}
socketley::ctl::command("start api_*");    // start all
socketley::ctl::command("stats api_*");    // query all
socketley::ctl::command("stop api_*");     // stop all
socketley::ctl::command("remove api_*");   // remove all
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: cache operations"
.nf
socketley::ctl::create("cache", "store", "-p 6379 --mode admin -s");
socketley::ctl::cache_set("store", "user:1", "alice");
auto r = socketley::ctl::cache_get("store", "user:1");
printf("user:1 = %s\\n", r.data.c_str());  // "alice"
socketley::ctl::cache_del("store", "user:1");
socketley::ctl::cache_flush("store", "/tmp/store.bin");
.fi
.SS Tier 2: Embed Engine (build from source)
Embed the full io_uring event loop and all four runtime types in your own binary.
Maximum performance with no IPC overhead. Requires
.B libsocketley_sdk.a
and link flags
.BR "-luring -lssl -lcrypto [-lluajit]" .
.PP
Headers:
.B socketley/core.h
(event_loop, runtime_manager, enums),
.B socketley/server.h ,
.B socketley/client.h ,
.B socketley/proxy.h ,
.B socketley/cache.h .
.PP
Define
.B SOCKETLEY_NO_LUA
to build without LuaJIT (omit
.B -lluajit
from link flags).
.PP
.B "High-Level Wrapper Classes (recommended)"
.PP
The
.B socketley::server\fR, \fBsocketley::client\fR, \fBsocketley::proxy\fR, and \fBsocketley::cache
classes wrap the raw engine API into a fluent, chainable interface.
All boilerplate (event loop, runtime manager, signal handling) is internal.
.PP
.B "socketley::server"
.nf
server(uint16_t port)

// Config (all return server& for chaining)
tls(cert, key)         // terminates TLS on this listener
client_ca(ca)          // mutual TLS on it: clients must present a certificate
                       // (tls_ca(ca) is the older spelling; it also sets tls)
max_connections(n)     rate_limit(r)
global_rate_limit(r)   idle_timeout(s)       lua(path)
mode(m)                udp()                 http_dir(path)
http_cache()           upstream(addr)        master_pw(pw)
drain()                group(g)              tick_interval(ms)

// Callbacks (return server& for chaining)
on_start(void())                             on_stop(void())
on_connect(void(int fd))                     on_disconnect(void(int fd))
on_message(void(int fd, string_view msg))    on_tick(void(double dt_ms))

// Actions
send(fd, msg)       broadcast(msg)      disconnect(fd)
peer_ip(fd)         clients()           multicast(fds, msg)
set_data(fd,k,v)    get_data(fd,k)

// Lifecycle
start()   stop()

// Escape hatches (raw engine access)
instance() -> server_instance*
manager()  -> runtime_manager&
loop()     -> event_loop&
.fi
.PP
.B "socketley::client"
.nf
client(string_view host, uint16_t port)

// Config
tls()              // dial over TLS; the host from the constructor is pinned
                   // and the chain verified against the system trust store
tls_ca(ca)         // verify against that CA instead of the system store
client_cert(c, k)  // the certificate this client presents when the peer asks
                   // for one - the client half of mutual TLS; needs tls()
reconnect(max)     mode(m)            udp()
lua(path)          tick_interval(ms)

// Callbacks
on_start    on_stop    on_connect    on_disconnect
on_message(void(string_view msg))   on_tick

// Actions
send(msg)

// Lifecycle: start()  stop()
// Escape hatches: instance()  manager()  loop()
.fi
.PP
.B "socketley::proxy"
.nf
proxy(uint16_t port)

// Config
backend(addr)      strategy(s)        protocol(p)
tls(cert, key)     // terminates TLS on this proxy's listener
client_ca(ca)      // mutual TLS on it: clients must present a certificate
backend_ca(ca)     // trust store for the backend leg; turns that leg to TLS
client_cert(c, k)  // the certificate presented to a backend that asks
max_connections(n) idle_timeout(s)
lua(path)          group(g)

// Callbacks: on_start  on_stop
// Lifecycle: start()  stop()
// Escape hatches: instance()  manager()  loop()
.fi
.PP
.B "socketley::cache"
.nf
cache(uint16_t port)

// Config
persistent(path)   max_memory(bytes)   eviction(policy)
resp()             mode(m)
tls(cert, key)     // NOT IMPLEMENTED - refused, no handshake here
max_connections(n) idle_timeout(s)     replicate(target)
lua(path)          group(g)

// Callbacks: on_start  on_stop
// Lifecycle: start()  stop()
// Escape hatches: instance()  manager()  loop()
.fi
.PP
.B "Limitations:"
One
.B start()
per process (the signal handler points to one event_loop).
.B start()
blocks until SIGINT/SIGTERM. For multi-runtime or async patterns, use the raw API
via the escape hatches or directly.
.PP
.B "TLS: on a server and a proxy only."
.B server().tls()
and
.B proxy().tls()
terminate TLS on that runtime's listener, and
.BR proxy().backend_ca() ,
.B proxy().client_ca()
and
.B proxy().client_cert()
configure the proxy's two legs, exactly as the CLI flags do \(em
.B start()
goes through
.B runtime_manager::start
and reaches the same gate. On a
.B client
or
.B cache
builder the same entry points still store their arguments and are read by
nothing, and there
.B start()
refuses rather than listening in cleartext. See
.B TLS
under
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.PP
.B "Example: echo server (3 lines)"
.nf
#include <socketley/server.h>
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    socketley::server srv(9000);

    srv.on_connect([](int fd) {
        printf("client %d connected\\n", fd);
    });
    srv.on_message([&](int fd, std::string_view msg) {
        srv.send(fd, "echo: " + std::string(msg));
    });
    srv.on_disconnect([](int fd) {
        printf("client %d disconnected\\n", fd);
    });

    srv.start();
}
// g++ -std=c++23 echo.cpp -I. -Iinclude/linux \\
//     -Lbin/Release -lsocketley_sdk -luring -lssl -lcrypto
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: server with Lua scripting"
.nf
int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    socketley::server srv(9000);
    srv.lua(argv[1]);
    srv.start();
}
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: TCP proxy"
.nf
socketley::proxy px(8080);
px.backend("127.0.0.1:9001")
  .backend("127.0.0.1:9002")
  .protocol(protocol_tcp)
  .strategy(strategy_round_robin);
px.start();
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: Redis-compatible cache"
.nf
socketley::cache c(6379);
c.persistent("/var/data/store.dat")
 .resp()
 .max_memory(256 * 1024 * 1024)
 .eviction(evict_allkeys_lru);
c.start();
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: client with auto-reconnect"
.nf
socketley::client cli("127.0.0.1", 9000);
cli.reconnect(0);  // 0 = infinite
cli.on_connect([&](int) { cli.send("hello"); });
cli.on_message([](std::string_view msg) {
    printf("%.*s\\n", (int)msg.size(), msg.data());
});
cli.start();
.fi
.PP
.B "Raw Engine API (advanced)"
.PP
For multi-runtime processes or custom event loop control, use the raw types directly.
The wrapper classes use this API internally.
.PP
.B "Core pattern:"
create event_loop, create runtime_manager, create runtimes, configure, run, loop.
.PP
.B "C++ callbacks"
(alternative to Lua scripts, set on any runtime):
.nf
set_on_start(function<void()>)
set_on_stop(function<void()>)
set_on_connect(function<void(int fd)>)
set_on_disconnect(function<void(int fd)>)
set_on_client_message(function<void(int fd, string_view)>)
set_on_message(function<void(string_view)>)
set_on_tick(function<void(double dt_ms)>)
.fi
.PP
.B "Common runtime setters:"
.nf
set_port(uint16_t)            set_event_loop(event_loop*)
set_runtime_manager(mgr*)     set_cache_name(string_view)
// set_tls / set_cert_path / set_key_path / set_ca_path are honoured
// on a server runtime and refused on every other type (see LIMITATIONS).
set_max_connections(uint32_t)
set_rate_limit(double)        set_idle_timeout(uint32_t)
set_log_file(string_view)     set_group(string_view)
set_drain(bool)               load_lua_script(string_view)
.fi
.PP
.B "Server-specific:"
.nf
set_mode(mode_inout|mode_in|mode_out|mode_master)
set_udp(bool)          set_http_dir(string_view)
lua_broadcast(msg)     lua_send_to(fd, msg)
lua_disconnect(fd)     lua_peer_ip(fd)
lua_clients()          lua_multicast(fds, msg)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache-specific:"
.nf
set_mode(cache_mode_readonly|readwrite|admin)
set_persistent(path)   set_max_memory(bytes)
set_resp_forced(bool)  set_replicate_target(host:port)
.fi
.PP
.B "Proxy-specific:"
.nf
set_protocol(protocol_http|protocol_tcp)
set_strategy(strategy_round_robin|random|lua)
add_backend(addr)      clear_backends()
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: multi-runtime with raw API (server + cache)"
.nf
#include <socketley/server.h>
#include <socketley/cache.h>

// ... (event_loop + runtime_manager setup as above)

mgr.create(runtime_cache, "store");
auto* cache = static_cast<cache_instance*>(mgr.get("store"));
cache->set_port(6379);
cache->set_mode(cache_mode_admin);
cache->set_runtime_manager(&mgr);
cache->set_event_loop(&loop);

mgr.create(runtime_server, "api");
auto* srv = static_cast<server_instance*>(mgr.get("api"));
srv->set_port(9000);
srv->set_cache_name("store");  // clients can "cache set k v"
srv->set_runtime_manager(&mgr);
srv->set_event_loop(&loop);

mgr.start("store", loop);
mgr.start("api", loop);
loop.run();
.fi
.SS Cross-Platform SDK (Windows / macOS)
Header-only TCP clients for connecting to Socketley runtimes from non-Linux platforms.
Available in
.B include/windows/socketley/
and
.BR include/darwin/socketley/ .
.PP
.B socketley/ws_client.h
\(em WebSocket (RFC 6455) + raw TCP client for server runtimes.
Embedded SHA-1 and Base64 (no OpenSSL). Modes:
.BR ws_mode::websocket " and " ws_mode::raw_tcp .
.PP
.B socketley/cache_client.h
\(em text protocol client for cache runtimes. Supports all cache commands
(get, set, del, lpush, sadd, hset, expire, publish, subscribe, etc.).
Returns
.B cache_result
with
.IR ok ", " value ", " values ", " integer
fields.
.PP
Build with C++17, no external dependencies:
.RS
.nf
# MSVC
cl /std:c++17 /I include\\windows app.cpp

# clang++ (macOS)
clang++ -std=c++17 -I include/darwin app.cpp -o app

# g++ (Linux)
g++ -std=c++17 -I include/windows app.cpp -o app
.fi
.RE
.SS Tier 3: Daemon Attach (header-only)
.B socketley/attach.h
registers your own process as a runtime with a running daemon.
After attaching, your process shows up in
.BR "socketley ls" ,
.BR "socketley stats" ,
and
.BR "socketley ps" .
Fully self-contained (builds on control.h). Auto-deregisters on
.BR exit() .
.PP
.B "API:"
.nf
bool daemon_attach(name, type, port)  // register, auto-detach on exit
void daemon_detach(name)              // explicit deregister
void daemon_detach()                  // deregister attach'd name
.fi
.PP
.B "Example:"
.nf
#include <socketley/attach.h>
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    // Start your own TCP server (any framework)
    // ...bind, listen on port 8080...

    if (socketley::daemon_attach("myservice", "server", 8080))
        printf("registered with daemon\\n");
    else
        printf("daemon not running (standalone mode)\\n");

    // Your accept loop here...
    // atexit() automatically calls daemon_detach()
}
// g++ -std=c++17 myapp.cpp -Iinclude/linux -o myapp
.fi
.SS Mixed: Engine + Daemon Attach (Tier 2 + 3)
Embed the full engine for maximum performance, and register with the daemon
for fleet monitoring. Best of both worlds.
.PP
.nf
#include <socketley/server.h>
#include <socketley/attach.h>

socketley::server srv(9000);
srv.on_message([&](int fd, std::string_view msg) {
    srv.send(fd, msg);
});

// Register with daemon so socketley ls/stats can see it
socketley::daemon_attach("app_srv", "server", 9000);

srv.start();  // daemon sees the runtime
.fi
.PP
.B "When to use each tier:"
.PP
.TP
.B "Tier 1"
Orchestration scripts, deployment tools, monitoring, CI/CD.
.TP
.B "Tier 2"
Custom daemons, game servers, IoT gateways, performance-critical services.
.TP
.B "Tier 3"
Legacy services, third-party processes, fleet visibility for external apps.
.TP
.B "Mixed"
Embedded engine with daemon monitoring \(em maximum performance with fleet management.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.SS Out\-of\-process proxies
.B \-\-out\-of\-process
is the first step of moving the proxy out of the daemon, and what it does
not yet cover is worth stating plainly.
.PP
A worker holds no runtime manager, so the cross\-runtime parts of the Lua
management API \(em creating, listing or sending to other runtimes, and
cache access by name \(em find nothing from inside one. Connection and
request callbacks run normally against the worker's own state. Interactive
mode
.RB ( "start \-i" )
is not offered for a proxy on either path.
.PP
Cross\-runtime pub/sub dispatch does not reach a worker: the daemon
delivers it by calling into each instance, and a placeholder has no
subscribers of its own.
.PP
The figures the daemon reports for a worker are as recent as the worker's
last publish \(em a quarter of a second, and refreshed on demand before
.B stats
answers. An in\-process runtime's are read live. The values agree; their
age does not.
.PP
Only the proxy can be split. The other runtimes still reach across
runtime boundaries through pointers and cannot be moved until they route
from pushed data the way the proxy now does.
.SS TLS
.B "Every runtime type speaks TLS: a server's and a cache's own listener, a proxy \(em on both of its legs \(em and a client runtime's dial."
Created with
.BR "\-\-tls \-\-cert " file " \-\-key " file ,
a
.B server
accepts, handshakes and moves records itself. OpenSSL never touches the
socket: each connection gets a memory BIO pair, the ciphertext is carried in
and out by the same io_uring submissions as every other byte in the daemon,
and the handshake advances one completion at a time so nothing blocks the
event loop. Protocol detection, WebSocket, HTTP and RESP all run on the
decrypted stream, so
.B wss://
and
.B https://
work on a listener configured this way.
.BI \-\-ca " file"
adds mutual TLS \(em a client must present a certificate signed by that CA.
.PP
A
.B proxy
runs the same record layer on both of its legs. The same three flags terminate
TLS on its listening port and
.BI \-\-client\-ca " file"
makes it demand a certificate from its callers; an
.B https://
backend address \(em or
.BR \-\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
or
.BR \-\-client\-key ,
which apply to every backend \(em takes the backend leg to TLS, with
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.B \-\-client\-key
presenting a certificate to a backend that asks for one. A TLS backend
connection is always verified, against
.B \-\-ca
or the system store, with the endpoint's name checked and TLS 1.2 as the
minimum version; there is no option to turn that off. Two consequences worth
knowing: such connections are never pooled, so each request pays for a
handshake, and
.B "\-\-health\-check http"
is refused against a TLS backend because a plaintext probe would mark every
backend unhealthy.
.PP
A
.B client
runtime runs the dialling half of that same record layer on its outbound
socket. Created with
.BR \-\-tls ,
it puts a ClientHello on the wire as soon as it has a socket and advances the
handshake one completion at a time, exactly as the other two do; the line
framing and the Lua callbacks above it see the same plaintext they always did.
The peer is verified against
.BI \-\-ca " file"
or the system store, and the certificate must be valid for the host in
.B \-t
\(em matched against its DNS SAN, or its IP SAN for an address literal.
.BI \-\-cert " file"
and
.BI \-\-key " file"
present a certificate to a peer that asks for one. There is no skip-verify
option, by design. A peer that fails either check costs the socket: the client
drops it and re-dials on its usual backoff, and never retries in cleartext.
.PP
A
.B cache
terminates TLS on its own listener with the same three flags a server uses, and
.BI \-\-ca " file"
demands a client certificate there in the same way. RESP is auto-detected from
the first byte of the decrypted stream, so an existing Redis client speaks the
protocol it always did, one layer up, and the line protocol works on the same
port.
.PP
.B "What a cache still cannot do over TLS is replicate."
Replication is not a listener and is not encrypted by anything: a follower
dials its leader itself, in cleartext, and on a leader a promoted follower is
written to by a second writer that goes around the record layer by design. So
the combination is refused rather than half-honoured \(em
.B \-\-tls
together with
.B \-\-replicate
is rejected at
.BR create ,
by
.BR edit " and " import ,
and again at every
.BR start ,
so a state file carrying both leaves the runtime
.B failed
rather than coming up quietly wrong; and a TLS cache asked to become a leader
by a
.B REPLICATE
command answers with an error instead of promoting itself. A cache that is
encrypted for its clients and cleartext for its replicas would be worse than
one that is honestly cleartext, because only the first of those looks safe.
.PP
Two further combinations are refused for the same reason:
.B \-\-udp
on a client or a server (DTLS is not implemented) and, on a server,
.B \-\-upstream
(the upstream connection would still be made in cleartext). A proxy in
.B \-\-protocol tcp
mode terminates TLS on its listener like any other, and forwards the decrypted
stream to its backend; the zero-copy splice path is bypassed on any connection
with a session, because a splice would move bytes the record layer never saw.
.PP
One outbound exception exists and is not transport:
.B socketley.http()
in a Lua callback speaks HTTPS, with the certificate chain and the host
name verified and TLS 1.2 as the minimum version (see
.BR "Global Functions" ).
That is a client call a script makes; it encrypts nothing that socketley
itself serves or forwards.
.PP
Earlier releases accepted the TLS flags on every runtime type, stored them,
persisted them, showed them in
.BR "socketley inspect" ,
and then encrypted nothing. A proxy created with
.B "\-\-tls \-\-cert \-\-key"
answered a plaintext HTTP request with 200 on the port it claimed to encrypt,
and a proxy aimed at an HTTPS backend sent a plaintext request line the
backend logged as a failed handshake. An operator asking for encryption was
told they had it. Both of those are now transport rather than decoration; the
roles that still have no handshake go on refusing the flags.
.PP
Everything that is not implemented therefore fails closed, and so does every
configuration that is incomplete:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR \-\-tls ", " \-\-cert ", " \-\-key " and " \-\-ca
on a
.B cache
runtime, and
.BR \-\-client\-ca ", " \-\-client\-cert " and " \-\-client\-key
on anything but a proxy, are rejected by
.B create
with exit status 1 and a message naming the flag.
.IP \(bu 2
on a
.BR server ", " client " or " proxy
runtime the same flags are accepted, but only as a complete set.
.B \-\-tls
without a certificate and key on a
.BR server " or " proxy ,
or a certificate or trust store without
.B \-\-tls
on any of the three, is refused and no runtime is created \(em either would
describe a listener that could only serve cleartext, or a dial that would be
made in it. On a
.B client
.B \-\-tls
alone is complete, but half a
.BR \-\-cert / \-\-key
pair is refused: it would present nothing while the flags said a certificate
was configured. On a proxy,
.B \-\-client\-ca
without
.B \-\-tls
and half a
.BR \-\-client\-cert / \-\-client\-key
pair are refused on the same reasoning.
.IP \(bu 2
flag-based
.B edit
applies the same rule, and restores the previous TLS settings if the edit
would leave them incomplete. It is a subset of
.BR create ,
so most flags it does not accept come back as
.BR "unknown flag" ;
these do not, because changing TLS on a live runtime is exactly what an
operator would be attempting.
.IP \(bu 2
.B import
applies the same rule to the JSON document's fields.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR start ", " reload
and the daemon's own boot-time restore refuse a runtime carrying a
configuration that cannot be honoured \(em which is how one restored from a
state file written by an older release is caught. Nothing is brought up; the
runtime goes to
.BR failed .
.IP \(bu 2
a certificate or key that cannot be read, or a key that does not match its
certificate, fails the runtime at start. The port is never bound.
.PP
To clear the settings from a runtime whose configuration is refused, dump it,
delete the TLS fields, and import it back (see
.BR import ).
.PP
The embedded C++ SDK reaches the same gate: its
.B start()
goes through
.BR runtime_manager::start ,
so
.B server().tls(cert, key)
and
.B proxy().tls(cert, key)
terminate TLS exactly as the daemon-managed runtime does, and the same call on
a client or cache fails the runtime instead of running in cleartext.
.PP
For encrypted transport on a
.BR client " or " cache
\(em or on a server's upstream connections \(em terminate TLS in a front-end
proxy (nginx, HAProxy, stunnel) and have it forward over loopback or an
otherwise trusted network path. A socketley
.B proxy
is now one of the things that can play that part.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Error (invalid command, runtime not found, rejected TLS flag, etc.).
.TP
.B 2
Fatal error.
